x$ approaches $-\\infty,$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow -\\infty } f(x) = 2.\n$$\n\nOn the right side of the graph, as the values of $x$ increase and approach $\\infty,$ the graph of the function grows without bound to $\\infty$ as well. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to \\infty } f(x) = \\infty.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-2986a890-b241-5a06-ba6c-578dfc9139a6" :question/id #uuid "2986a890-b241-5a06-ba6c-578dfc9139a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-337600" :question/problem "![](images/b3/b35bd1000242bcbc00fcd5e1cd04d8f3b13e563f2df72234b438827949ad4ef5.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Therefore\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we see that as $x$ decreases to $-\\infty ,$ the graph of the function moves closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=2.$\n\nTherefore, as $x$ approaches $-\\infty ,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches $2,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bebf9261-924a-5e1a-88b8-24fd5ab23e17"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bebf9261-924a-5e1a-88b8-24fd5ab23e17" :answer-field/id #uuid "bebf9261-924a-5e1a-88b8-24fd5ab23e17" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-1104f803-e925-52a4-a645-afee67efaa1e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1104f803-e925-52a4-a645-afee67efaa1e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1104f803-e925-52a4-a645-afee67efaa1e" :answer/id #uuid "1104f803-e925-52a4-a645-afee67efaa1e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ecf33790-880b-54f0-875d-ea7461ed14bb" :question/id #uuid "ecf33790-880b-54f0-875d-ea7461ed14bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-35812" :question/problem "![](images/d6/d6448b3b7899a447d4259d6e4d60ea33262306755485aafa7604e13b3709fca0.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Find ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we see that as $x$ decreases to $-\\infty ,$ the graph of the function moves closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=-2.$\n\nTherefore, as $x$ approaches $-\\infty ,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches $-2,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=-2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-040f1458-944f-575a-95cf-2a20a2ad718a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-040f1458-944f-575a-95cf-2a20a2ad718a" :answer-field/id #uuid "040f1458-944f-575a-95cf-2a20a2ad718a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1b222c95-61f8-57e8-8e0f-3573a20419c6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1b222c95-61f8-57e8-8e0f-3573a20419c6" "answer-cb74d9a8-f4e5-5ef1-bcfb-8deb3748409e" "answer-777f48fb-1b50-589b-a9a3-8a2623788e9a" "answer-b2785696-f2d6-51fd-8b99-47190908ed88" "answer-9d4ed3c0-e5e6-53eb-bdc3-642a46c636f4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1b222c95-61f8-57e8-8e0f-3573a20419c6" :answer/id #uuid "1b222c95-61f8-57e8-8e0f-3573a20419c6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cb74d9a8-f4e5-5ef1-bcfb-8deb3748409e" :answer/id #uuid "cb74d9a8-f4e5-5ef1-bcfb-8deb3748409e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-777f48fb-1b50-589b-a9a3-8a2623788e9a" :answer/id #uuid "777f48fb-1b50-589b-a9a3-8a2623788e9a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b2785696-f2d6-51fd-8b99-47190908ed88" :answer/id #uuid "b2785696-f2d6-51fd-8b99-47190908ed88" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\infty " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9d4ed3c0-e5e6-53eb-bdc3-642a46c636f4" :answer/id #uuid "9d4ed3c0-e5e6-53eb-bdc3-642a46c636f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9e0ab428-de64-5dc9-82ec-d9e388ec395a" :question/id #uuid "9e0ab428-de64-5dc9-82ec-d9e388ec395a" :question/math-academy-id "q-337625" :question/problem "![](images/71/717e9728ec64332ffa0f115b8698fe5babac2b6c11e1764aeb28494b89a0c141.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Therefore\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} \n$\\,$ and $\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "On the left side of the graph, we see that as the values of $x$ decrease and approach $-\\infty $, the graph of the function moves closer and closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=3.$ \n\nConsequently, $f(x)$ approaches the value $3$ as $x$ approaches $-\\infty ,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=3.\n$$\n\nOn the right side of the graph, as the values of $x$ increase and approach $\\infty ,$ the graph of the function goes without bound to $-\\infty .$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=-\\infty .\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7bb33194-2026-55f0-9bc6-4eaf63699f95" "answer-field-b8a64255-f025-5f63-b69a-4e7b54b34285"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7bb33194-2026-55f0-9bc6-4eaf63699f95" :answer-field/id #uuid "7bb33194-2026-55f0-9bc6-4eaf63699f95" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f98bb391-8a6f-5d3f-9c06-ff3bb2ee4d9a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f98bb391-8a6f-5d3f-9c06-ff3bb2ee4d9a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f98bb391-8a6f-5d3f-9c06-ff3bb2ee4d9a" :answer/id #uuid "f98bb391-8a6f-5d3f-9c06-ff3bb2ee4d9a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b8a64255-f025-5f63-b69a-4e7b54b34285" :answer-field/id #uuid "b8a64255-f025-5f63-b69a-4e7b54b34285" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-fe1fed8a-f0ff-54d2-b48c-4e615c5eab79" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fe1fed8a-f0ff-54d2-b48c-4e615c5eab79"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fe1fed8a-f0ff-54d2-b48c-4e615c5eab79" :answer/id #uuid "fe1fed8a-f0ff-54d2-b48c-4e615c5eab79" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-20650e5d-9531-58b0-8986-a410d28ac857" :question/id #uuid "20650e5d-9531-58b0-8986-a410d28ac857" :question/math-academy-id "q-102473" :question/problem "![](images/d3/d3d381842539a44e53d77697b18f0cb51552996c885a41e6681d56cba5025428.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Find  ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "On the left side of the graph, we see that as the values of $x$ decrease and approach $-\\infty $, the graph of the function moves closer and closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=0.$ \n\nConsequently, $f(x)$ approaches the value $0$ as $x$ approaches $-\\infty ,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=0.\n$$\n\nOn the right side of the graph, as the values of $x$ increase and approach $\\infty ,$ the graph of the function grows without bound to $\\infty $ as well. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=\\infty .\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d514cf12-4a07-554b-a399-ec37796f1ec8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d514cf12-4a07-554b-a399-ec37796f1ec8" :answer-field/id #uuid "d514cf12-4a07-554b-a399-ec37796f1ec8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-475186f4-dc87-58a6-9081-13a54b4a2d9c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d3886d3c-89e6-5a05-8ffc-24fd67a5d7b2" "answer-3c61804f-0426-5bd0-879c-10e37f3d6812" "answer-609c690a-1985-5867-88ea-6530c846e51c" "answer-81c2b701-5db9-575d-9e08-953094b78ec5" "answer-475186f4-dc87-58a6-9081-13a54b4a2d9c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d3886d3c-89e6-5a05-8ffc-24fd67a5d7b2" :answer/id #uuid "d3886d3c-89e6-5a05-8ffc-24fd67a5d7b2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=-\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c61804f-0426-5bd0-879c-10e37f3d6812" :answer/id #uuid "3c61804f-0426-5bd0-879c-10e37f3d6812" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=-\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=\\infty $" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-609c690a-1985-5867-88ea-6530c846e51c" :answer/id #uuid "609c690a-1985-5867-88ea-6530c846e51c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=0$ and ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=\\infty $" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-81c2b701-5db9-575d-9e08-953094b78ec5" :answer/id #uuid "81c2b701-5db9-575d-9e08-953094b78ec5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=-\\infty $" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-475186f4-dc87-58a6-9081-13a54b4a2d9c" :answer/id #uuid "475186f4-dc87-58a6-9081-13a54b4a2d9c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-be5e5f56-4a60-5c32-8894-f56ae09b72b4" :question/id #uuid "be5e5f56-4a60-5c32-8894-f56ae09b72b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-102470" :question/problem "![](images/07/07b3922c7b14ed6bb5c613f81485fc4e155232fe0958805b9437c7ca1b4e608a.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Find  ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "On the left side of the graph, we see that as the values of $x$ decrease and approach $-\\infty $, the graph of the function grows without bound to $\\infty .$\n\nConsequently, $f(x)$ approaches $\\infty $ as $x$ approaches $-\\infty ,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nOn the right side of the graph, as the values of $x$ increase and approach $\\infty ,$\nthe graph of the function moves closer and closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=-2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ea243ce2-45fd-5cec-817c-777d580842aa"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ea243ce2-45fd-5cec-817c-777d580842aa" :answer-field/id #uuid "ea243ce2-45fd-5cec-817c-777d580842aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4deb45f9-98ed-520e-8fe2-1fe96ff3f473" :answer-field/choices ["answer-432e6bf4-e493-5434-ae9a-e0016232e17c" "answer-31b42c29-5b27-5782-9407-a827fe74eada" "answer-4deb45f9-98ed-520e-8fe2-1fe96ff3f473" "answer-cd2fc4cf-c91f-5e2b-ba44-7663cb5c839f" "answer-dae4ec75-3a91-5668-b380-0a4af9b966a7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-432e6bf4-e493-5434-ae9a-e0016232e17c" :answer/id #uuid "432e6bf4-e493-5434-ae9a-e0016232e17c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=-2$ and ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=-\\infty $" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31b42c29-5b27-5782-9407-a827fe74eada" :answer/id #uuid "31b42c29-5b27-5782-9407-a827fe74eada" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=-2$ and ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4deb45f9-98ed-520e-8fe2-1fe96ff3f473" :answer/id #uuid "4deb45f9-98ed-520e-8fe2-1fe96ff3f473" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=-2$ and ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=\\infty $" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd2fc4cf-c91f-5e2b-ba44-7663cb5c839f" :answer/id #uuid "cd2fc4cf-c91f-5e2b-ba44-7663cb5c839f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=0$ and ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=\\infty $" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dae4ec75-3a91-5668-b380-0a4af9b966a7" :answer/id #uuid "dae4ec75-3a91-5668-b380-0a4af9b966a7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=-\\infty $" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125714 :tutorial/content "Until now, we have considered limits at individual points: \n\n$\\lim\\limits_{x \\to a} f(x)$ is the value that $f(x)$ approaches as $x$ approaches the point $a.$ \n\nHowever, the idea of a limit extends beyond individual points. For instance, it might also be the case that $f(x)$ approaches some value as $x$ gets bigger and bigger, growing without bound.\n\nTo illustrate, consider the function $f(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x} + 1$ whose graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/71/71fdfd68ce32097cac17da935f0422b471b28d804f4108c62783d0ffc1e96f24.png)\n\nOn the right side of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets bigger and bigger, $f(x)$ approaches the horizontal asymptote $y=1.$ In other words, as $x$ increases to infinity ($\\infty$), the value of $f(x)$ approaches a limit of $1.$ We can write this symbolically as\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to \\infty } f(x) = 1.\n$$\n\nLikewise, on the left side of the graph, as $x$ decreases to negative infinity ($-\\infty$), the value of $f(x)$ approaches the same horizontal asymptote $y=1.$ Consequently, we also have that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to -\\infty } f(x) = 1.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124686 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125504 :step/math-academy-id 655037 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125315 :step/math-academy-id 655038 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125317 :step/math-academy-id 655042 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125505 :step/math-academy-id 655043 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "activity-0ac63a17-8eba-58fd-81f6-c5dfcfca821d" :activity/id #uuid "0ac63a17-8eba-58fd-81f6-c5dfcfca821d" :activity/title "Optimizing Multivariable Functions Using Lagrange Multipliers" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050688 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-0ef9d217-5318-56ef-a70a-b3b1bdd62fac" "step-eef0b459-bbf6-5186-8922-6b8989aa4de0" "step-ad8a33d6-0b0a-5996-8da1-7927904bffbd" "step-f98db47a-48c0-5d3a-ba0a-051c09b08ff1" "step-a66e406f-6c08-5429-b179-f50c725ecf90"] :activity/first-step "step-0ef9d217-5318-56ef-a70a-b3b1bdd62fac" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-0ef9d217-5318-56ef-a70a-b3b1bdd62fac" :step/id #uuid "0ef9d217-5318-56ef-a70a-b3b1bdd62fac" :step/content 17592186199017 :step/next "step-eef0b459-bbf6-5186-8922-6b8989aa4de0" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-eef0b459-bbf6-5186-8922-6b8989aa4de0" :step/id #uuid "eef0b459-bbf6-5186-8922-6b8989aa4de0" :step/content 17592186362342 :step/next "step-ad8a33d6-0b0a-5996-8da1-7927904bffbd" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ad8a33d6-0b0a-5996-8da1-7927904bffbd" :step/id #uuid "ad8a33d6-0b0a-5996-8da1-7927904bffbd" :step/content 17592186364648 :step/next "step-f98db47a-48c0-5d3a-ba0a-051c09b08ff1" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-f98db47a-48c0-5d3a-ba0a-051c09b08ff1" :step/id #uuid "f98db47a-48c0-5d3a-ba0a-051c09b08ff1" :step/content 17592186281543 :step/next "step-a66e406f-6c08-5429-b179-f50c725ecf90" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-a66e406f-6c08-5429-b179-f50c725ecf90" :step/id #uuid "a66e406f-6c08-5429-b179-f50c725ecf90" :step/content 17592186446815 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186396387 :question/problem "The local truncation error (LTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}-(Δx)^{4}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.3,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.1,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.3$ to $Δx=0.1,$ the LTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221404 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-83896362-1e2d-5431-a36e-61683a703ea6"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-83896362-1e2d-5431-a36e-61683a703ea6" :question/id #uuid "83896362-1e2d-5431-a36e-61683a703ea6" :question/math-academy-id "q-334865" :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $4.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.20$ produces a global truncation error (GTE) of $e(0.20)≈0.234\\,375.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.08,$ what GTE should we expect?\n\n$e(0.08)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.20$ to $0.08.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.20}{0.08}=2.5.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $4,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{4}=(2.5)^{4}=39.0625.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.08,$ the expected GTE is\n\n$$\ne(0.08)≈\\frac{0.234\\,375}{39.0625}=0.006.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-abfff1ee-ddd9-547e-a06a-6674882e92fe"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-abfff1ee-ddd9-547e-a06a-6674882e92fe" :answer-field/id #uuid "abfff1ee-ddd9-547e-a06a-6674882e92fe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-50111e51-5d29-56d4-8ecb-f3dd1e391393" :answer-field/choices ["answer-50111e51-5d29-56d4-8ecb-f3dd1e391393"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-50111e51-5d29-56d4-8ecb-f3dd1e391393" :answer/id #uuid "50111e51-5d29-56d4-8ecb-f3dd1e391393" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.006" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-4178e725-dd3e-5e50-9f29-c459bc20e2d5" :activity/id #uuid "4178e725-dd3e-5e50-9f29-c459bc20e2d5" :activity/title "Order of Numerical Methods" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186051034 :activity/course 17592186046645 :activity/steps ["step-34d37626-02af-5288-bb95-4617c8db7b8e" "step-e27c9be6-c330-57b1-8fbe-55a6c777f228" "step-dbfdc4d8-99e3-5c2c-bda4-2d7cd0687229" "step-6425cffd-f7ac-54c6-9836-0e3778651904" "step-bf194409-ddab-577c-8513-a28b02b55d4a"] :activity/first-step "step-34d37626-02af-5288-bb95-4617c8db7b8e" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-34d37626-02af-5288-bb95-4617c8db7b8e" :step/id #uuid "34d37626-02af-5288-bb95-4617c8db7b8e" :step/content 17592186308493 :step/next "step-e27c9be6-c330-57b1-8fbe-55a6c777f228" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e27c9be6-c330-57b1-8fbe-55a6c777f228" :step/id #uuid "e27c9be6-c330-57b1-8fbe-55a6c777f228" :step/content 17592186308664 :step/next "step-dbfdc4d8-99e3-5c2c-bda4-2d7cd0687229" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-dbfdc4d8-99e3-5c2c-bda4-2d7cd0687229" :step/id #uuid "dbfdc4d8-99e3-5c2c-bda4-2d7cd0687229" :step/content 17592186396387 :step/next "step-6425cffd-f7ac-54c6-9836-0e3778651904" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-6425cffd-f7ac-54c6-9836-0e3778651904" :step/id #uuid "6425cffd-f7ac-54c6-9836-0e3778651904" :step/content 17592186535065 :step/next "step-bf194409-ddab-577c-8513-a28b02b55d4a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-bf194409-ddab-577c-8513-a28b02b55d4a" :step/id #uuid "bf194409-ddab-577c-8513-a28b02b55d4a" :step/content "question-83896362-1e2d-5431-a36e-61683a703ea6" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162042 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-47b88835-9941-5b00-8cef-a75a2de591b3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-47b88835-9941-5b00-8cef-a75a2de591b3" :question/id #uuid "47b88835-9941-5b00-8cef-a75a2de591b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-363832" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  select the correct options in the computations of the matrix $U$ below.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}=10$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\n\nNow, we compute the $1$st column of $U.$ It is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${u}_{1}=\\,\\,$ |  | $\\frac{4}{5}$ |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal.\n\nHence, we must find vectors $u=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}]^{T}$ such that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to \n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nChoosing ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{3}$ as free variables, the general solution is given by:\n\n|  |  | ${x}_{1}$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-5}} |  |  |\n\nthat we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince the two linearly independent solutions are already mutually orthogonal, we only need to normalize them. Hence, we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-6}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-7}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-8}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                $0$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-9}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=10$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{1}+\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,3{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{1}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{3}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables.\n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we only need to normalize them. This gives the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & -\\frac{3}{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fa7da9c9-345c-5889-a4c