ield/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-32399941-82c0-5d5a-a312-a1e1997e4e44" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d7220373-d119-5591-850e-24ed7375c3d9" "answer-e9f5d90b-26ca-53c0-9a57-4c841c19feec" "answer-32399941-82c0-5d5a-a312-a1e1997e4e44" "answer-70e9f5a4-a29f-52f3-bf78-f68e822a4cd2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d7220373-d119-5591-850e-24ed7375c3d9" :answer/id #uuid "d7220373-d119-5591-850e-24ed7375c3d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{2\\sqrt{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9f5d90b-26ca-53c0-9a57-4c841c19feec" :answer/id #uuid "e9f5d90b-26ca-53c0-9a57-4c841c19feec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-32399941-82c0-5d5a-a312-a1e1997e4e44" :answer/id #uuid "32399941-82c0-5d5a-a312-a1e1997e4e44" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{2\\sqrt{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-70e9f5a4-a29f-52f3-bf78-f68e822a4cd2" :answer/id #uuid "70e9f5a4-a29f-52f3-bf78-f68e822a4cd2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163233 :tutorial/content "We can compute a singular value decomposition (SVD) of any matrix! The algorithm for computing an SVD of a rectangular $m\\times n$ matrix $(m\\neq n)$ is similar to the algorithm for square matrices.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/f6/f65e0eee905da85ce0be4bbf39546c2b31c2bac082ff00a6a1d54fd5b89a4a4e.svg)\n\nNotice that $A$ is a ${\\color{blue}{3}}\\times {\\color{red}{2}}$ matrix. So, to compute the SVD of $A,$ we must write\n\n$$\nA=U \\Sigma V^T,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\Sigma$ is a ${\\color{blue}{3}}\\times {\\color{red}{2}}$ matrix with the singular values of $A$ (ordered from largest to smallest) on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal ${\\color{blue}{3}} \\times {\\color{blue}{3}}$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal ${\\color{red}{2}} \\times {\\color{red}{2}}$ matrix.\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.**  Find the singular values of $A.$ Computing the eigenvalues of $A^TA,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = 4, \\qquad \\lambda_2 = 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are \n\n$$\n\\sigma_1 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_1} =  2,  \\qquad \\sigma_2 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_2} =  \\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T A$). Seeking non-zero solutions of $(A^T \\! A - \\lambda_1 I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ and $(A^T \\! A - \\lambda_2 I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the unit eigenvectors \n![](images/07/07085f021218ff17060b3c1a9099e1cabad24b3d12269f97f1fe50d49b970f6b.svg)\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A.$ Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the first two columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\mathbf u_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute $\\mathbf u_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $U$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix. However, we do not have the third singular value, so we can't use the same formula to find the third column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector \n![](images/71/71f89dbcee33bce284b3085b872fdb9da2cc41166fe8a0bd26ef0bb5fe6876aa.svg) \nsuch that \n![](images/9e/9edc744372650e86c27f31ef15ac575cc8203803bdc5fe5730390727fd37146a.svg)\nSolving this system, we get the following nonzero normalized solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U,$ $\\Sigma,$ and $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/c2/c2bbd771d886da6b3a369b7e03345d1be75c18c26c66590598581d2d99590524.svg)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119950 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-225e6266-f488-590a-bcc1-b7110b1ab57c" "question-6e05b66e-ca2b-55a8-b1d5-5df4f7e7120d" "question-a5329a3e-b8b4-504e-8c17-47d6c0564352" "question-5f82136c-d352-5849-8678-e3857b7e43c6" "question-498cc30d-95e0-5d5e-8baf-d608afabcdc8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119536 :question/problem "What is the recursive formula for the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n1, \\: 4, \\: 16, \\: \\ldots\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The recursive formula for a geometric sequence with common ratio $r$ takes the form\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = r \\cdot a_{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the first term is $a_1={\\color{blue}1},$ and we can calculate the common ratio $r$ as\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{a_2}{a_1} = \\dfrac 4 1 = {\\color{red}4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the recursive formula is\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = {\\color{red}4} \\cdot a_{n} , \\qquad a_1 = {\\color{blue}1}, \\qquad n\\geq 1.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119537 :question/problem "What is the $4$th term of the geometric sequence given below?\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = -3 a_n,\\qquad a_1 = 2,\\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the recursive formula to compute the terms of the sequence up to the $4$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =2 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =-3{a}_{1}=-3(2)=-6 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =-3{a}_{2}=-3(-6)=18 \\\\ {a}_{4} & =-3{a}_{3}=-3(18)=-54\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a_4 = -54.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119584 :question/problem "What is the $5$th term of the geometric sequence given below?\n\n$$\nf(n+1) = -5f(n),\\quad f(1) = -1,\\quad  n\\geq 1\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the recursive formula to compute the terms of the sequence up to the $4$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =-1 \\\\ f(2) & =-5f(1)=-5(-1)=5 \\\\ f(3) & =-5f(2)=-5(5)=-25 \\\\ f(4) & =-5f(3)=-5(-25)=125 \\\\ f(5) & =-5f(4)=-5(125)=-625\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(5)=-625.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119538 :question/problem "Which of the three sequences below are geometric sequences?\n\nI. $10, \\: 20, \\: 30, \\: 40, \\: \\dots$\n\nII. $a_{n+1} = \\dfrac{1}{6}a_n,\\quad a_1 = 36,\\quad n\\geq 1$\n\nIII. $f(n+1) = 2f(n)-1,\\quad f(1) =2,\\quad  n\\geq 1$" :question/worked-solution "A sequence is geometric if there is a common ratio between its terms. Let's check each sequence in turn.\n\n- Sequence I is not geometric because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{20}{10} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{30}{20} & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence II is a geometric sequence. The recursive rule $a_{n+1} = \\dfrac{1}{6}a_n$ tells us that each term is $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ times the previous term, so the common ratio is $r=\\dfrac{1}{6}.$\n\n- Sequence III is not a geometric sequence. To see this, we can calculate the first $3$ terms of the sequence and then compute the ratios of consecutive terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =2 \\\\ f(2) & =2\\cdot f(1)-1=2\\cdot 2-1=3 \\\\ f(3) & =2\\cdot f(2)-1=2\\cdot 3-1=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first two pairs of consecutive terms have different ratios:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(2)}{f(1)}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{f(3)}{f(2)}=\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only sequence II is a geometric sequence." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-225e6266-f488-590a-bcc1-b7110b1ab57c" :question/id #uuid "225e6266-f488-590a-bcc1-b7110b1ab57c" :question/math-academy-id "q-249796" :question/problem "Consider the following geometric sequence:\n\n$$\n-48,\\,36,\\,-27,\\,…\n$$\n\nThe recursive formula for this geometric sequence is\n\n${a}_{n+1}=${{field-1}}$,\\,{a}_{1}=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,n\\ge 1.$" :question/worked-solution "The recursive formula for a geometric sequence with common ratio $r$ takes the form\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=r\\cdot {a}_{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{1}=-48,$ and we can calculate the common ratio $r$ as\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{36}{-48}=-\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the recursive formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{3}{4}{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=-48,\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a57da2c0-90ed-5f07-addb-e7aacf7566d8" "answer-field-9f053b0a-afab-5b22-80de-3501f84a15e6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a57da2c0-90ed-5f07-addb-e7aacf7566d8" :answer-field/id #uuid "a57da2c0-90ed-5f07-addb-e7aacf7566d8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-6dc75461-f680-52f9-bdb9-64d7afe41e1a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6dc75461-f680-52f9-bdb9-64d7afe41e1a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6dc75461-f680-52f9-bdb9-64d7afe41e1a" :answer/id #uuid "6dc75461-f680-52f9-bdb9-64d7afe41e1a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}{a}_{n}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9f053b0a-afab-5b22-80de-3501f84a15e6" :answer-field/id #uuid "9f053b0a-afab-5b22-80de-3501f84a15e6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-2acc437e-a412-581d-8e81-dfe5cfc62ad9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2acc437e-a412-581d-8e81-dfe5cfc62ad9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2acc437e-a412-581d-8e81-dfe5cfc62ad9" :answer/id #uuid "2acc437e-a412-581d-8e81-dfe5cfc62ad9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-48" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6e05b66e-ca2b-55a8-b1d5-5df4f7e7120d" :question/id #uuid "6e05b66e-ca2b-55a8-b1d5-5df4f7e7120d" :question/math-academy-id "q-109804" :question/problem "What is the recursive formula for the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n3,\\,-12,\\,48,\\,…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The recursive formula for a geometric sequence with common ratio $r$ takes the form\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=r\\cdot {a}_{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{1}=3,$ and we can calculate the common ratio $r$ as\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{-12}{3}=-4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the recursive formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=-4{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=3,\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7d7dfc4c-3be2-5c52-8f14-4925b82dc4d7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7d7dfc4c-3be2-5c52-8f14-4925b82dc4d7" :answer-field/id #uuid "7d7dfc4c-3be2-5c52-8f14-4925b82dc4d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5e627b36-0bed-53c2-87b0-11792e481c81" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a09ab400-53e2-5d77-99dd-4639394319aa" "answer-5e627b36-0bed-53c2-87b0-11792e481c81" "answer-cb91e927-0186-52d3-b60a-f7d417fe23d0" "answer-f2a7e502-4c1c-5f69-ba12-c209563b85be" "answer-59109616-a72d-56f4-b284-2356b9a3a252"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a09ab400-53e2-5d77-99dd-4639394319aa" :answer/id #uuid "a09ab400-53e2-5d77-99dd-4639394319aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=-2{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=6,\\,n\\ge 1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e627b36-0bed-53c2-87b0-11792e481c81" :answer/id #uuid "5e627b36-0bed-53c2-87b0-11792e481c81" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=-4{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=3,\\,n\\ge 1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cb91e927-0186-52d3-b60a-f7d417fe23d0" :answer/id #uuid "cb91e927-0186-52d3-b60a-f7d417fe23d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=3,\\,n\\ge 1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f2a7e502-4c1c-5f69-ba12-c209563b85be" :answer/id #uuid "f2a7e502-4c1c-5f69-ba12-c209563b85be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=-4,\\,n\\ge 1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-59109616-a72d-56f4-b284-2356b9a3a252" :answer/id #uuid "59109616-a72d-56f4-b284-2356b9a3a252" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}={a}_{n}-4,\\,{a}_{1}=7,\\,n\\ge 1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a5329a3e-b8b4-504e-8c17-47d6c0564352" :question/id #uuid "a5329a3e-b8b4-504e-8c17-47d6c0564352" :question/math-academy-id "q-249793" :question/problem "Consider the following geometric sequence:\n\n$$\n64,\\,32,\\,16,\\,…\n$$\n\nThe recursive formula for this geometric sequence is\n\n${a}_{n+1}=${{field-1}}$,\\,{a}_{1}=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,n\\ge 1.$" :question/worked-solution "The recursive formula for a geometric sequence with common ratio $r$ takes the form\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=r\\cdot {a}_{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{1}=64,$ and we can calculate the common ratio $r$ as\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{32}{64}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the recursive formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=64,\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7685adc9-c06d-5b60-9d9b-882b9ed5af74" "answer-field-c9ff6506-1647-592e-b5f6-dfcf283ece96"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7685adc9-c06d-5b60-9d9b-882b9ed5af74" :answer-field/id #uuid "7685adc9-c06d-5b60-9d9b-882b9ed5af74" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-e39963bd-2dfe-5a7e-adaa-2c2cf677ac70" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e39963bd-2dfe-5a7e-adaa-2c2cf677ac70"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e39963bd-2dfe-5a7e-adaa-2c2cf677ac70" :answer/id #uuid "e39963bd-2dfe-5a7e-adaa-2c2cf677ac70" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}a_n" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c9ff6506-1647-592e-b5f6-dfcf283ece96" :answer-field/id #uuid "c9ff6506-1647-592e-b5f6-dfcf283ece96" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-530993ed-b33b-597f-ad91-770ac1be6f70" :answer-field/choices ["answer-530993ed-b33b-597f-ad91-770ac1be6f70"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-530993ed-b33b-597f-ad91-770ac1be6f70" :answer/id #uuid "530993ed-b33b-597f-ad91-770ac1be6f70" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "64" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5f82136c-d352-5849-8678-e3857b7e43c6" :question/id #uuid "5f82136c-d352-5849-8678-e3857b7e43c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-109803" :question/problem "What is the recursive formula for the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n5,\\,1,\\,\\frac{1}{5},\\,…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The recursive formula for a geometric sequence with common ratio $r$ takes the form\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=r\\cdot {a}_{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{1}=5,$ and we can calculate the common ratio $r$ as\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the recursive formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{1}{5}{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=5,\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-256ce9ac-b6f2-58d9-9923-d36fccd40be2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-256ce9ac-b6f2-58d9-9923-d36fccd40be2" :answer-field/id #uuid "256ce9ac-b6f2-58d9-9923-d36fccd40be2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5a6271c4-dc33-5178-8880-d84db8f789e5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e1e388cb-bd96-503a-915d-cbba5975a2c4" "answer-6ed046c8-7602-5017-b313-39c70d6930b3" "answer-5a6271c4-dc33-5178-8880-d84db8f789e5" "answer-6b8951a6-e073-51d8-932a-75d271fd5be2" "answer-5fd17d56-55b0-5789-8b8e-7b8949679984"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e1e388cb-bd96-503a-915d-cbba5975a2c4" :answer/id #uuid "e1e388cb-bd96-503a-915d-cbba5975a2c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=5{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=5,\\,n\\ge 1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6ed046c8-7602-5017-b313-39c70d6930b3" :answer/id #uuid "6ed046c8-7602-5017-b313-39c70d6930b3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=2,\\,n\\ge 1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5a6271c4-dc33-5178-8880-d84db8f789e5" :answer/id #uuid "5a6271c4-dc33-5178-8880-d84db8f789e5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{1}{5}{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=5,\\,n\\ge 1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6b8951a6-e073-51d8-932a-75d271fd5be2" :answer/id #uuid "6b8951a6-e073-51d8-932a-75d271fd5be2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}={a}_{n}+5,\\,{a}_{1}=5,\\,n\\ge 1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5fd17d56-55b0-5789-8b8e-7b8949679984" :answer/id #uuid "5fd17d56-55b0-5789-8b8e-7b8949679984" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}={a}_{n}+\\frac{1}{5},\\,{a}_{1}=1,\\,n\\ge 1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-498cc30d-95e0-5d5e-8baf-d608afabcdc8" :question/id #uuid "498cc30d-95e0-5d5e-8baf-d608afabcdc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-44900" :question/problem "What is the recursive formula for the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n24,\\,-12,\\,6,\\,…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The recursive formula for a geometric sequence with common ratio $r$ takes the form\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=r\\cdot {a}_{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{1}=24,$ and we can calculate the common ratio $r$ as\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{-12}{24}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the recursive formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=24,\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e6960de4-32f2-5e23-a67e-c1fd22c3c7b3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e6960de4-32f2-5e23-a67e-c1fd22c3c7b3" :answer-field/id #uuid "e6960de4-32f2-5e23-a67e-c1fd22c3c7b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-eabbcd8b-e3e1-568e-8bb4-7faa54e7eac9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-786b34ba-15b9-5365-b95c-9713f3204748" "answer-0d07ba6b-560e-5f76-bba4-ab3cc421726a" "answer-eabbcd8b-e3e1-568e-8bb4-7faa54e7eac9" "answer-6f636481-d088-57f3-b4e1-1fff7ed219c3" "answer-198dd29e-d1f9-5b83-b5d6-a3755e2179ed"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-786b34ba-15b9-5365-b95c-9713f3204748" :answer/id #uuid "786b34ba-15b9-5365-b95c-9713f3204748" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=-2{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=24,\\,n\\ge 1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d07ba6b-560e-5f76-bba4-ab3cc421726a" :answer/id #uuid "0d07ba6b-560e-5f76-bba4-ab3cc421726a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=-2{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{2}=24,\\,n\\ge 1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eabbcd8b-e3e1-568e-8bb4-7faa54e7eac9" :answer/id #uuid "eabbcd8b-e3e1-568e-8bb4-7faa54e7eac9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=24,\\,n\\ge 1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6f636481-d088-57f3-b4e1-1fff7ed219c3" :answer/id #uuid "6f636481-d088-57f3-b4e1-1fff7ed219c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=24,\\,n\\ge 1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-198dd29e-d1f9-5b83-b5d6-a3755e2179ed" :answer/id #uuid "198dd29e-d1f9-5b83-b5d6-a3755e2179ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}={a}_{n}-36,\\,{a}_{1}=24,\\,n\\ge 1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186120994 :tutorial/content "The common ratio ![](images/3a/3a4ef1c1b261a53f9c45d0c90f2d44b155125fe1e7b1359406830111b4846f2f.svg) of a geometric sequence is found by dividing any term by the term immediately before it:\n![](images/64/641727086c22a06619c11617fae703a715530b8436e05ea22ad57cfffdbf9e8f.svg)\n\nTherefore, the **recursive formula for a geometric sequence** with common ratio ![](images/3a/3a4ef1c1b261a53f9c45d0c90f2d44b155125fe1e7b1359406830111b4846f2f.svg) takes the form\n![](images/c6/c68961a8641390e8d86b86ec557512300a53f424912bed25e9939126e5d71c07.svg)\n\nThe above formula states that to get to the next term in the sequence, we multiply the previous term by ![](images/b2/b2e0c6bf822f391aab6c59cc540176f139c5c69f5a6f54ae9c81d451defe9c9f.svg)\n\nTo illustrate, consider the geometric sequence below:\n\n![](images/7c/7ceb288dea6b0c885bae7014d7170c923afb2d38b8ba6cb338cd63c32e6af5c0.svg)\n\nThe first term is ![](images/82/82447c5e47fb052201522cca2359b821307def74212b7ce26debbfd8875f4dc2.svg) and we can calculate the common ratio ![](images/3a/3a4ef1c1b261a53f9c45d0c90f2d44b155125fe1e7b1359406830111b4846f2f.svg) as\n\n![](images/d2/d2c615fcab22a64d8f610b7ff36d28190b787c456e9e4099d8f5be2522dafd9c.svg)\n\nTherefore, the recursive formula is\n\n![](images/9d/9d7aaea9acc235b192240da83d3e53d9d62ee6e67e128df01dba6bd0d1ced4ad.svg)\n\nWhen writing out our final answer for the recursive formula, we should also state the first term and the range for ![](images/c2/c2475d72e6ec36042292de6c0160930174172b2386148d823a70e59fddfb93c2.svg)\n\nTo check that the recursive formula is correct, we can use it to generate the next few terms of the series:\n\n![](images/b3/b397de9b364d11653d651b7fa2d6ddf422ede5fca1692596f527e3e293bafb27.svg)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186120025 :activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068843 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-90e45430-ff4c-59cd-b8f8-806fae001939" "question-407bfc4b-29e1-5ad8-862e-1c4f1b286f41"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-90e45430-ff4c-59cd-b8f8-806fae001939" :question/id #uuid "90e45430-ff4c-59cd-b8f8-806fae001939" :question/math-academy-id "q-53171" :question/problem "![](images/db/db1b39e3f00fd6bb64ed738fb12213f4775d2bd7d098dfa9d0efec2192d7a64f.png)\n\nGiven that $r$, $s$ and $t$ in the above diagram are parallel, find $m∠1.$" :question/worked-solution "Consider $∠2$ and $∠3$ in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/a7/a775b7c9ce63aa5b9f8c03fb91e7018d2f1edde5a2a2acdb099ae5ba53a874e4.png)\n\nNote that $∠2$ and the angle whose measure is ${75}^{∘}$ are corresponding angles formed by the same transversal. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm∠2={75}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, note that $∠3$ and the angle whose measure is ${50}^{∘}$ are corresponding angles formed by the same transversal. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm∠3={50}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nLet's add these measures to our diagram:\n\n![](images/01/01462502a4fbe0b25e489cc611200beb47cd926b8a055b69b7279aa0f301850b.png)\n\nThe angles $∠1$, $∠2,$ and $∠3$ combine to give a straight angle. So, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠1+m∠2+m∠3 & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠1+{75}^{∘}+{50}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠1+{125}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠1 & ={55}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8d96c227-6f23-514a-8097-fbbd94755da1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8d96c227-6f23-514a-8097-fbbd94755da1" :answer-field/id #uuid "8d96c227-6f23-514a-8097-fbbd94755da1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-facc42be-49b3-5141-9e80-3358d50482bd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-10bb692e-36e7-518e-934f-7f2348cb810a" "answer-4552359d-6331-5661-8b66-027569a2f7c1" "answer-facc42be-49b3-5141-9e80-3358d50482bd" "answer-4903221c-fcab-5a64-8b23-188c2c939e88" "answer-df394d85-dc28-5c29-8329-dc16aae0f557"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-10bb692e-36e7-518e-934f-7f2348cb810a" :answer/id #uuid "10bb692e-36e7-518e-934f-7f2348cb810a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4552359d-6331-5661-8b66-027569a2f7c1" :answer/id #uuid "4552359d-6331-5661-8b66-027569a2f7c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{50}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-facc42be-49b3-5141-9e80-3358d50482bd" :answer/id #uuid "facc42be-49b3-5141-9e80-3358d50482bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{55}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4903221c-fcab-5a64-8b23-188c2c939e88" :answer/id #uuid "4903221c-fcab-5a64-8b23-188c2c939e88" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df394d85-dc28-5c29-8329-dc16aae0f557" :answer/id #uuid "df394d85-dc28-5c29-8329-dc16aae0f557" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{70}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-407bfc4b-29e1-5ad8-862e-1c4f1b286f41" :question/id #uuid "407bfc4b-29e1-5ad8-862e-1c4f1b286f41" :question/math-academy-id "q-117549" :question/problem "![](images/cc/ccdbd48b6cdf40205fe15a0b52553926aa4ba5e66e5a29bcf9ce6ae4164154a4.png)\n\nGiven that $r$, $s$ and $t$ in the above diagram are parallel, find $m∠v.$" :question/worked-solution "Consider $∠2$ and $∠3$ in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/50/50d1e010a8279bf8f59a5e57b4ee4d4b744af7e431351bb17397e123aa79c59a.png)\n\nNote that $∠2$ and the angle whose measure is ${100}^{∘}$ are corresponding angles formed by the same transversal. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm∠2={100}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, note that $∠3$ and the angle whose measure is ${36}^{∘}$ are corresponding angles formed by the same transversal. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm∠3={36}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nLet's add these measures to our diagram:\n\n![](images/ce/ce9944a3fa28177bd0882fccd53d544159b6f041c570e7037cac7db31103130e.png)\n\nThe angles $∠v$, $∠2,$ and $∠3$ combined give a straight angle. So, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠v+m∠2+m∠3 & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠v+{100}^{∘}+{36}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠v+{136}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠v & ={44}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a6e4fe6a-97c0-55f8-aa37-67b07ae4b10b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a6e4fe6a-97c0-55f8-aa37-67b07ae4b10b" :answer-field/id #uuid "a6e4fe6a-97c0-55f8-aa37-67b07ae4b10b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c7711a92-f975-5159-8094-830ed6cf3bee" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e5a98074-8d83-5bec-84ca-c92ae7444892" "answer-c7cd1ffe-fc48-5e5c-b643-f8b5e62deab4" "answer-c7711a92-f975-5159-8094-830ed6cf3bee" "answer-0746683d-8797-5f2b-95dc-71f40b0506c4" "answer-8418df2d-a794-5d2d-8b95-e9c9f6f2cc0d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5a98074-8d83-5bec-84ca-c92ae7444892" :answer/id #uuid "e5a98074-8d83-5bec-84ca-c92ae7444892" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{31}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c7cd1ffe-fc48-5e5c-b643-f8b5e62deab4" :answer/id #uuid "c7cd1ffe-fc48-5e5c-b643-f8b5e62deab4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{36}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c7711a92-f975-5159-8094-830ed6cf3bee" :answer/id #uuid "c7711a92-f975-5159-8094-830ed6cf3bee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{44}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0746683d-8797-5f2b-95dc-71f40b0506c4" :answer/id #uuid "0746683d-8797-5f2b-95dc-71f40b0506c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{48}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8418df2d-a794-5d2d-8b95-e9c9f6f2cc0d" :answer/id #uuid "8418df2d-a794-5d2d-8b95-e9c9f6f2cc0d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{54}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068839 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6d28d4f3-c7e7-5aa9-96b5-112441d6c1ac"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-6d28d4f3-c7e7-5aa9-96b5-112441d6c1ac" :question/id #uuid "6d28d4f3-c7e7-5aa9-96b5-112441d6c1ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-117567" :question/problem "![](images/47/4745f9e651eb50c345550e83ee82eb957cd28fd82e3398b667e4285bb512194c.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ in the figure above given that $\\overset{⟷}{AB}∥\\overset{⟷}{CD}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the angles $∠JID$ and $∠EJB$ are corresponding angles, they are congruent and therefore have the same measure:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠JID & =m∠EJB \\\\ 4x+{25}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘}-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have an equation that we can solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+{25}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘}-x \\\\ 5x+{25}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 5x & ={155}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{5x}{5} & =\\frac{{155}^{∘}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{5}x}{\\cancel{5}} & ={31}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={31}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5ce27563-7842-5bd7-8582-3e404e021713"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5ce27563-7842-5bd7-8582-3e404e021713" :answer-field/id #uuid "5ce27563-7842-5bd7-8582-3e404e021713" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a5702a25-959c-54e4-8b48-d733d466e381" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cfaa1e72-858f-5fb1-b6b1-a45409e1345a" "answer-6aa510db-507e-5d1c-ac87-823b20e24290" "answer-68b20937-06fa-5ee7-9f09-8c2eff9900a8" "answer-a5702a25-959c-54e4-8b48-d733d466e381" "answer-479d9a01-9deb-5a09-aa6b-934264ad1bfb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cfaa1e72-858f-5fb1-b6b1-a45409e1345a" :answer/id #uuid "cfaa1e72-858f-5fb1-b6b1-a45409e1345a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{25}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6aa510db-507e-5d1c-ac87-823b20e24290" :answer/id #uuid "6aa510db-507e-5d1c-ac87-823b20e24290" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{28}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-68b20937-06fa-5ee7-9f09-8c2eff9900a8" :answer/id #uuid "68b20937-06fa-5ee7-9f09-8c2eff9900a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{29}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a5702a25-959c-54e4-8b48-d733d466e381" :answer/id #uuid "a5702a25-959c-54e4-8b48-d733d466e381" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{31}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-479d9a01-9deb-5a09-aa6b-934264ad1bfb" :answer/id #uuid "479d9a01-9deb-5a09-aa6b-934264ad1bfb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{35}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-49957f7a-4afb-5f72-8d90-9be2de6b3a44" :activity/id #uuid "49957f7a-4afb-5f72-8d90-9be2de6b3a44" :activity/title "Corresponding Angles" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049587 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-9cbce6de-8a3c-5419-8c14-0e187454a2b5" "step-6804ab59-29d8-51c1-a53a-33027de8ef79" "step-e3e36f95-7ba1-5506-be93-9e4d488f2a59"] :activity/first-step "step-9cbce6de-8a3c-5419-8c14-0e187454a2b5" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-9cbce6de-8a3c-5419-8c14-0e187454a2b5" :step/id #uuid "9cbce6de-8a3c-5419-8c14-0e187454a2b5" :step/content "question-90e45430-ff4c-59cd-b8f8-806fae001939" :step/next "step-6804ab59-29d8-51c1-a53a-33027de8ef79" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-6804ab59-29d8-51c1-a53a-33027de8ef79" :step/id #uuid "6804ab59-29d8-51c1-a53a-33027de8ef79" :step/content "question-407bfc4b-29e1-5ad8-862e-1c4f1b286f41" :step/next "step-e3e36f95-7ba1-5506-be93-9e4d488f2a59" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e3e36f95-7ba1-5506-be93-9e4d488f2a59" :step/id #uuid "e3e36f95-7ba1-5506-be93-9e4d488f2a59" :step/content "question-6d28d4f3-c7e7-5aa9-96b5-112441d6c1ac" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221988 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr></tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & {\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 0 & 2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=2t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}r(2t)={e}^{2t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(2t)={e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}2t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\n2t{e}^{2t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t}(1-2t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =2{e}^{2t}t+{e}^{2t}(1-2t) \\\\  & ={e}^{2t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t & ={e}^{2t}t \\\\  & =t{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186220584 :answer-field/correct "answer-10f335ea-9d53-5bd8-a277-0e71096d1701" :answer-field/choices ["answer-10f335ea-9d53-5bd8-a277-0e71096d1701"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-10f335ea-9d53-5bd8-a277-0e71096d1701" :answer/id #uuid "10f335ea-9d53-5bd8-a277-0e71096d1701" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "e^{2t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186220586 :answer-field/correct "answer-227c788b-a841-56fc-9c67-21ee4721d3f8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-227c788b-a841-56fc-9c67-21ee4721d3f8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-227c788b-a841-56fc-9c67-21ee4721d3f8" :answer/id #uuid "227c788b-a841-56fc-9c67-21ee4721d3f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "te^{2t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186220583 :answer-field/correct "answer-4475a7f4-c95c-5c55-8109-0e9ea886a2a8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4475a7f4-c95c-5c55-8109-0e9ea886a2a8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4475a7f4-c95c-5c55-8109-0e9ea886a2a8" :answer/id #uuid "4475a7f4-c95c-5c55-8109-0e9ea886a2a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186220585 :answer-field/correct "answer-d4192bd6-a9b5-5e3c-a850-538acc11b68f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d4192bd6-a9b5-5e3c-a850-538acc11b68f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d4192bd6-a9b5-5e3c-a850-538acc11b68f" :answer/id #uuid "d4192bd6-a9b5-5e3c-a850-538acc11b68f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "e^{2t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-f960835e-e823-56aa-a86d-984171474118" :activity/id #uuid "f960835e-e823-56aa-a86d-984171474118" :activity/title "Fundamental Matrices" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050228 :activity/course 17592186046645 :activity/steps ["step-c89da063-d346-5af9-92b8-fb8eb176e13a" "step-c739f31a-33e2-5b79-9d44-a58e2e368af7" "step-64943c28-d0a3-53a2-88a5-d9adefa020e7" "step-f58d9680-20f3-5d4a-86d9-25e71b4b53a0" "step-b2a1b6ac-99f1-5614-aa30-4a7585d5f1a5"] :activity/first-step "step-c89da063-d346-5af9-92b8-fb8eb176e13a" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-c89da063-d346-5af9-92b8-fb8eb176e13a" :step/id #uuid "c89da063-d346-5af9-92b8-fb8eb176e13a" :step/content 17592186221988 :step/next "step-c739f31a-33e2-5b79-9d44-a58e2e368af7" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-c739f31a-33e2-5b79-9d44-a58e2e368af7" :step/id #uuid "c739f31a-33e2-5b79-9d44-a58e2e368af7" :step/content 17592186308609 :step/next "step-64943c28-d0a3-53a2-88a5-d9adefa020e7" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-64943c28-d0a3-53a2-88a5-d9adefa020e7" :step/id #uuid "64943c28-d0a3-53a2-88a5-d9adefa020e7" :step/content 17592186308611 :step/next "step-f58d9680-20f3-5d4a-86d9-25e71b4b53a0" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-f58d9680-20f3-5d4a-86d9-25e71b4b53a0" :step/id #uuid "f58d9680-20f3-5d4a-86d9-25e71b4b53a0" :step/content 17592186396895 :step/next "step-b2a1b6ac-99f1-5614-aa30-4a7585d5f1a5" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b2a1b6ac-99f1-5614-aa30-4a7585d5f1a5" :step/id #uuid "b2a1b6ac-99f1-5614-aa30-4a7585d5f1a5" :step/content 17592186365731 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186278475 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-x+\\sqrt{2}y)=-1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-x+\\sqrt{2}y)=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+(-1)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{4}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot r\\text{ }\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\,]}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{3}(-1)\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186278486 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2x+2y)=-2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2x+2y)=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(x+y)\\cdot \\sqrt{1+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(x+y)\\cdot \\sqrt{9}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{∬}_{D}\\,(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{∬}_{D}\\,(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A & =3{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =3{∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{∬}_{Δ}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-a7e447dc-efe8-5990-94ca-1d71d0aed212" :activity/id #uuid "a7e447dc-efe8-5990-94ca-1d71d0aed212" :activity/title "Surface Integrals Over Cartesian Surfaces" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049883 :activity/course 17592186046660 :activity/steps ["step-64217169-0cc8-5c96-92a7-60e0327f3a58" "step-c6dc5e58-d1a8-5fb6-8b34-42dd7fce411d" "step-6efb6b15-91ab-572a-9dfc-ab077408f2c0" "step-e699603d-7b06-57fa-a1ac-56875ac67a88" "step-6ffb9c84-ee99-5da3-ab01-c527c118e32a"] :activity/first-step "step-64217169-0cc8-5c96-92a7-60e0327f3a58" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-64217169-0cc8-5c96-92a7-60e0327f3a58" :step/id #uuid "64217169-0cc8-5c96-92a7-60e0327f3a58" :step/content 17592186278475 :step/next "step-c6dc5e58-d1a8-5fb6-8b34-42dd7fce411d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-c6dc5e58-d1a8-5fb6-8b34-42dd7fce411d" :step/id #uuid "c6dc5e58-d1a8-5fb6-8b34-42dd7fce411d" :step/content 17592186278486 :step/next "step-6efb6b15-91ab-572a-9dfc-ab077408f2c0" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-6efb6b15-91ab-572a-9dfc-ab077408f2c0" :step/id #uuid "6efb6b15-91ab-572a-9dfc-ab077408f2c0" :step/content 17592186278488 :step/next "step-e699603d-7b06-57fa-a1ac-56875ac67a88" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e699603d-7b06-57fa-a1ac-56875ac67a88" :step/id #uuid "e699603d-7b06-57fa-a1ac-56875ac67a88" :step/content 17592186415981 :step/next "step-6ffb9c84-ee99-5da3-ab01-c527c118e32a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-6ffb9c84-ee99-5da3-ab01-c527c118e32a" :step/id #uuid "6ffb9c84-ee99-5da3-ab01-c527c118e32a" :step/content 17592186106064 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186080003 :answer-field/choices 17592186080004] [:db/retract 17592186080003 :answer-field/choices 17592186080005] [:db/retract 17592186080003 :answer-field/choices 17592186080006] [:db/retract 17592186080003 :answer-field/choices 17592186080007] [:db/retract 17592186080003 :answer-field/choices 17592186080008] {:db/id 17592186083337 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e2c56edd-8fb8-5a8e-abe8-142f14623ed7" "question-f16d560c-09a5-5adf-953f-3ef2b521c424" "question-abc49f5c-860a-5246-8fd2-ba05f76f3a6e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083139 :question/worked-solution "![](images/b9/b970e539af5b695644486ad4eb7c21558c334ffd33c1df1a2343fac493d84389.png)\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The points in the scatter plot seem to follow a straight line with a *negative* slope. So, we have a *negative* correlation.\n\n- The points are scattered quite far from the corresponding trend line. So, we have a *weak* correlation.\n\nTherefore, the variables have a *weak negative* linear correlation." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083338 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-09efb2e6-1e80-53f4-bf82-55db52baf7d8" "question-2e20c6e9-8a1f-5520-84a5-13e0b1715760" "question-9f7eb28b-e6d3-58a7-ad9a-3a6681ca1ee4" "question-e6c59cf1-e1f3-5c09-adfe-bd7d9e53be52"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083140 :question/problem "![](images/4e/4e3c58da0dc7f326497d9a7c1327d6d40f98684089694f9de88f21a64f4b4824.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the correlation coefficient for the data in the scatter plot above?\n\nI. $\\rho =-0.8$\n\nII. $\\rho= 0.3$\n\nIII. $\\rho = 0.9$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the linear correlation coefficient $\\rho$ satisfies the inequality\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\rho \\leq 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\rho = 1$ indicates a perfect positive correlation, and $\\rho = -1$ indicates a perfect negative correlation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our scatter plot.\n\n![](images/06/06dcc617bbbc07fea756f35975146a238bd4cb9c4ce437f1b9300a7c05ded7b6.png)\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The points in the scatter plot seem to follow a straight line with a *positive* slope. So, the correlation coefficient must be *positive*. Thus, we disregard the following value:\n\n$$\n\\rho = -0.8\n$$\n\n- The points lie pretty close to the corresponding trend line. So, the correlation coefficient must be closer to $1.$ Thus, we disregard the following value:\n\n$$\n\\rho = 0.3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the best approximation of the correlation coefficient from the given options is $\\rho= 0.9.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083339 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-fdb0f84a-e85a-559c-9fae-e9b6176fb94c" "question-bc9c7169-1131-5a87-93ac-6b79642c5534" "question-06e32974-1b71-50c8-93f5-929432644d25" "question-d339220f-f17c-55cf-8865-ba521fdebae7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083141 :question/problem "It is known that there is a linear correlation between the variables $x$ and $y.$ Given that the correlation coefficient is $\\rho\\approx -0.85,$ sketch a possible scatter plot that corresponds to this situation." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the linear correlation coefficient $\\rho$ satisfies the inequality\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\rho \\leq 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\rho = 1$ indicates a perfect positive correlation, and $\\rho = -1$ indicates a perfect negative correlation.\n\nTherefore, since $\\rho \\approx -0.85,$ there is a *strong negative* linear correlation in the data.\n\n- The *negative* linear correlation means that the data points should be scattered along a line with a *negative* slope.\n\n- Since the correlation is *strong*, most of the points should lie *close* to the trend line.\n\nTherefore, this situation could correspond to the following scatter plot:\n\n![](images/ad/ad8ff5e49649b5e788b83eb28fa26afae61072d5e63026631a9cd1891be14a0c.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-e2c56edd-8fb8-5a8e-abe8-142f14623ed7" :question/id #uuid "e2c56edd-8fb8-5a8e-abe8-142f14623ed7" :question/math-academy-id "q-134103" :question/problem "![](images/04/04fbb73cfa465f4cb052f3347a8651bae48140441c4fbfa753d2bd86ac41dfe8.png)\n\nA group of students is interviewed. The scatter plot above shows the correspondence between the hours they spent playing video games daily the week before the literature exam and the grade they got on the exam. Which of the following best describes the correlation between the variables in the scatter plot?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/91/91d928d021c8715a24fdb7dc262bec53c30626d8649a2ac451c94f9c0ecc0db0.png)\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The points in the scatter plot seem to follow a straight line with a *negative* slope. So, we have a *negative* correlation.\n\n- The points lie pretty close to the corresponding trend line. So, we have a *strong* correlation.\n\nTherefore, the variables have a *strong negative* linear correlation." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c2702c34-c7e9-5325-9741-8ce3622c97cd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c2702c34-c7e9-5325-9741-8ce3622c97cd" :answer-field/id #uuid "c2702c34-c7e9-5325-9741-8ce3622c97cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-548cb0fc-8599-5d8e-a01c-66d33541fbb9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-da723436-8cde-5bcc-ab9f-5d1691728692" "answer-548cb0fc-8599-5d8e-a01c-66d33541fbb9" "answer-8231fe35-d847-50c6-b767-316e740854a8" "answer-29391afd-e40c-555e-af97-50ca4ab507f6" "answer-7184b1f4-449d-5810-934f-45a18f1048e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da723436-8cde-5bcc-ab9f-5d1691728692" :answer/id #uuid "da723436-8cde-5bcc-ab9f-5d1691728692" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "no linear correlation" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-548cb0fc-8599-5d8e-a01c-66d33541fbb9" :answer/id #uuid "548cb0fc-8599-5d8e-a01c-66d33541fbb9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "strong negative linear correlation" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8231fe35-d847-50c6-b767-316e740854a8" :answer/id #uuid "8231fe35-d847-50c6-b767-316e740854a8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "strong positive linear correlation" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-29391afd-e40c-555e-af97-50ca4ab507f6" :answer/id #uuid "29391afd-e40c-555e-af97-50ca4ab507f6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "weak negative linear correlation" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7184b1f4-449d-5810-934f-45a18f1048e6" :answer/id #uuid "7184b1f4-449d-5810-934f-45a18f1048e6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "weak positive linear correlation" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f16d560c-09a5-5adf-953f-3ef2b521c424" :question/id #uuid "f16d560c-09a5-5adf-953f-3ef2b521c424" :question/math-academy-id "q-133914" :question/problem "![](images/95/952ca756e237387be25ee214ba6ea2907c9af7bd6d03e9f2810648a7eb367368.png)\n\nWhich of the following best describes the correlation between the variables in the scatter plot above?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/d9/d91ac29530bd2408ae92cf4ea815d1a155803b8144994184cbaca09a8c0f79e4.png)\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The points in the scatter plot seem to follow a straight line with a *negative* slope. So, we have a *negative* correlation.\n\n- The points are scattered quite far from the corresponding trend line. So, we have a *weak* correlation.\n\nTherefore, the variables have a *weak negative* linear correlation." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-806c7b23-25c0-565c-ae67-5936dc4941f1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-806c7b23-25c0-565c-ae67-5936dc4941f1" :answer-field/id #uuid "806c7b23-25c0-565c-ae67-5936dc4941f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-19a11638-5a2e-5378-9104-e808844efb1f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-92d3eaca-820e-5a0b-a3c3-5153f8613c52" "answer-0660e208-70e5-5b04-ad6a-42881363a6ec" "answer-e300029a-aa4d-5e7f-9e2e-d5e7ac4adb22" "answer-19a11638-5a2e-5378-9104-e808844efb1f" "answer-89f6ee56-ed02-5b9a-9754-8229c9143dff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-92d3eaca-820e-5a0b-a3c3-5153f8613c52" :answer/id #uuid "92d3eaca-820e-5a0b-a3c3-5153f8613c52" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "no linear correlation" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0660e208-70e5-5b04-ad6a-42881363a6ec" :answer/id #uuid "0660e208-70e5-5b04-ad6a-42881363a6ec" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "strong negative linear correlation" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e300029a-aa4d-5e7f-9e2e-d5e7ac4adb22" :answer/id #uuid "e300029a-aa4d-5e7f-9e2e-d5e7ac4adb22" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "strong positive linear correlation" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-19a11638-5a2e-5378-9104-e808844efb1f" :answer/id #uuid "19a11638-5a2e-5378-9104-e808844efb1f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "weak negative linear correlation" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-89f6ee56-ed02-5b9a-9754-8229c9143dff" :answer/id #uuid "89f6ee56-ed02-5b9a-9754-8229c9143dff" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "weak positive linear correlation" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083516 :question/worked-solution "![](images/6b/6b21527790fddecf89cd72a2e1d1f67338d21d82c1f3090536be973fcd60919a.png)\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The points in the scatter plot seem to follow a straight line with a *positive* slope. So, we have a *positive* correlation.\n\n- The points lie pretty close to the corresponding trend line. So, we have a *strong* correlation.\n\nTherefore, the variables have a *strong positive* linear correlation." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186079991 :answer-field/correct 17592186079992 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-abc49f5c-860a-5246-8fd2-ba05f76f3a6e" :question/id #uuid "abc49f5c-860a-5246-8fd2-ba05f76f3a6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-134099" :question/problem "![](images/25/2561ac832231961f439731ff16a5b918787b848fc41f77fb63e987c79d50f40b.png)\n\nA study is made on the grades of a group of high school students. The scatter plot above shows the relationship between grades obtained in the subjects of chemistry and mathematics. Which of the following best describes the correlation between the variables in the scatter plot?" :question/worked-solution "From the plot, it seems that the data points are scattered randomly. Therefore, there is no linear correlation." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-02e3d693-e281-56a8-a4da-4749c2f3df92"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-02e3d693-e281-56a8-a4da-4749c2f3df92" :answer-field/id #uuid "02e3d693-e281-56a8-a4da-4749c2f3df92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e934472d-0a94-531d-bdcd-4001307fafc7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e934472d-0a94-531d-bdcd-4001307fafc7" "answer-349057ee-b77b-5a2f-a3a1-1255acb11c2e" "answer-04c24679-f692-5bfb-9d7c-05f5dc96b029" "answer-69fd680b-f8f6-5f46-bd13-cd6083c3e0aa" "answer-45060158-a98c-561c-80cb-006ceb1d17b5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e934472d-0a94-531d-bdcd-4001307fafc7" :answer/id #uuid "e934472d-0a94-531d-bdcd-4001307fafc7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "no linear correlation" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-349057ee-b77b-5a2f-a3a1-1255acb11c2e" :answer/id #uuid "349057ee-b77b-5a2f-a3a1-1255acb11c2e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "strong negative linear correlation" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-04c24679-f692-5bfb-9d7c-05f5dc96b029" :answer/id #uuid "04c24679-f692-5bfb-9d7c-05f5dc96b029" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "strong positive linear correlation" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-69fd680b-f8f6-5f46-bd13-cd6083c3e0aa" :answer/id #uuid "69fd680b-f8f6-5f46-bd13-cd6083c3e0aa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "weak negative linear correlation" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45060158-a98c-561c-80cb-006ceb1d17b5" :answer/id #uuid "45060158-a98c-561c-80cb-006ceb1d17b5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "weak positive linear correlation" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083517 :question/worked-solution "From the plot, it seems that the data points are scattered randomly. Therefore, there is no linear correlation." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186079997 :answer-field/correct 17592186079998 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083518 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the linear correlation coefficient $\\rho $ satisfies the inequality\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\rho \\le 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\rho =1$ indicates a perfect positive correlation, and $\\rho =-1$ indicates a perfect negative correlation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our scatter plot.\n\n![](images/91/91d605a297f2fd3c90c59592cc08184c4201f04ebc69bfc285e4261af8e918d2.png)\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The points in the scatter plot seem to follow a straight line with a *negative* slope. So, the correlation coefficient must be *negative*. Thus, we disregard the following values:\n\n$$\n\\rho ≈0.9,\\,\\rho ≈0.1,\\,\\rho ≈0\n$$\n\n- The points lie pretty close to the corresponding trend line. So, the correlation coefficient must be closer to $-1.$ Thus, we disregard the following value:\n\n$$\n\\rho ≈-0.2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the best approximation of the correlation coefficient must be $\\rho ≈-0.8.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186080003 :answer-field/correct "answer-cb7ee60a-05da-569e-8c17-f1335eac64cb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f5909bb2-0a90-52d9-b686-d0e61412bfbe" "answer-cb7ee60a-05da-569e-8c17-f1335eac64cb" "answer-0c7cb220-5afc-5b19-a7bf-aeaaba188e81" "answer-bf05904c-18b3-52af-a5c3-6e120a983a80" "answer-2ecf7776-f06c-5178-9d27-b89b7e8cfb8a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5909bb2-0a90-52d9-b686-d0e61412bfbe" :answer/id #uuid "f5909bb2-0a90-52d9-b686-d0e61412bfbe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\rho ≈-0.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cb7ee60a-05da-569e-8c17-f1335eac64cb" :answer/id #uuid "cb7ee60a-05da-569e-8c17-f1335eac64cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\rho ≈-0.8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0c7cb220-5afc-5b19-a7bf-aeaaba188e81" :answer/id #uuid "0c7cb220-5afc-5b19-a7bf-aeaaba188e81" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\rho ≈0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bf05904c-18b3-52af-a5c3-6e120a983a80" :answer/id #uuid "bf05904c-18b3-52af-a5c3-6e120a983a80" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2ecf7776-f06c-5178-9d27-b89b7e8cfb8a" :answer/id #uuid "2ecf7776-f06c-5178-9d27-b89b7e8cfb8a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-09efb2e6-1e80-53f4-bf82-55db52baf7d8" :question/id #uuid "09efb2e6-1e80-53f4-bf82-55db52baf7d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-134122" :question/problem "![](images/f2/f2f569fe07310e8bd068cda0152b296d7b1189efec03720a0d3a043a7ab52d21.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows the relationship between the age of some people and the average number of cups of coffee they consume daily. \nWhich of the following is the best approximation of the correlation coefficient?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the linear correlation coefficient $\\rho $ satisfies the inequality\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\rho \\le 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\rho =1$ indicates a perfect positive correlation, and $\\rho =-1$ indicates a perfect negative correlation.\n\nFrom the plot, it seems that the data points are scattered randomly. Therefore, there is no linear correlation.\n\nTherefore, the best approximation of the correlation coefficient must be $\\rho ≈0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4fae3beb-3510-5939-afed-b54d6ee4d3a2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4fae3beb-3510-5939-afed-b54d6ee4d3a2" :answer-field/id #uuid "4fae3beb-3510-5939-afed-b54d6ee4d3a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e97f983b-8d60-5aec-9580-1d14d322c307" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7ef364a3-b3b6-5068-ab53-fbf996e0a6aa" "answer-ad977d26-9351-54cd-9449-7de02c055b6a" "answer-e97f983b-8d60-5aec-9580-1d14d322c307" "answer-01a7c6d4-0487-53da-a22e-734216f21437" "answer-9c99d273-eee7-551e-991e-7d2f5b565510"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ef364a3-b3b6-5068-ab53-fbf996e0a6aa" :answer/id #uuid "7ef364a3-b3b6-5068-ab53-fbf996e0a6aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\rho ≈-0.3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad977d26-9351-54cd-9449-7de02c055b6a" :answer/id #uuid "ad977d26-9351-54cd-9449-7de02c055b6a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\rho ≈-0.7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e97f983b-8d60-5aec-9580-1d14d322c307" :answer/id #uuid "e97f983b-8d60-5aec-9580-1d14d322c307" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\rho ≈0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-01a7c6d4-0487-53da-a22e-734216f21437" :answer/id #uuid "01a7c6d4-0487-53da-a22e-734216f21437" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9c99d273-eee7-551e-991e-7d2f5b565510" :answer/id #uuid "9c99d273-eee7-551e-991e-7d2f5b565510" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2e20c6e9-8a1f-5520-84a5-13e0b1715760" :question/id #uuid "2e20c6e9-8a1f-5520-84a5-13e0b1715760" :question/math-academy-id "q-134126" :question/problem "![](images/27/271e5f80ff6ab311bcdbc6b2725516b16a5c3fee63b6d219a59801c78d4f77e4.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows the relationship between the number of shirts and the number of pants sold in a store over a certain number of days. \nWhich of the following is the best approximation of the correlation coefficient?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the linear correlation coefficient $\\rho $ satisfies the inequality\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\rho \\le 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\rho =1$ indicates a perfect positive correlation, and $\\rho =-1$ indicates a perfect negative correlation.\n\nFrom the plot, it seems that the data points are scattered randomly. Therefore, there is no linear correlation.\n\nTherefore, the best approximation of the correlation coefficient must be $\\rho ≈0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-157cc70f-8bfd-56d9-b601-f1ea6f2f9206"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-157cc70f-8bfd-56d9-b601-f1ea6f2f9206" :answer-field/id #uuid "157cc70f-8bfd-56d9-b601-f1ea6f2f9206" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-975ac0a8-2d8e-501d-aae1-278a3552d5f3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e340584d-ae6e-5e84-a152-3f6a2121b262" "answer-b3b980a5-1b07-53a0-bd99-f5969d3971f0" "answer-975ac0a8-2d8e-501d-aae1-278a3552d5f3" "answer-647e1d76-8699-5f00-b950-f65a5d9a1e25" "answer-5fb66c80-1747-5cd6-b473-d39c5d64cf56"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e340584d-ae6e-5e84-a152-3f6a2121b262" :answer/id #uuid "e340584d-ae6e-5e84-a152-3f6a2121b262" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\rho ≈-0.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3b980a5-1b07-53a0-bd99-f5969d3971f0" :answer/id #uuid "b3b980a5-1b07-53a0-bd99-f5969d3971f0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\rho ≈-0.8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-975ac0a8-2d8e-501d-aae1-278a3552d5f3" :answer/id #uuid "975ac0a8-2d8e-501d-aae1-278a3552d5f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\rho ≈0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-647e1d76-8699-5f00-b950-f65a5d9a1e25" :answer/id #uuid "647e1d76-8699-5f00-b950-f65a5d9a1e25" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5fb66c80-1747-5cd6-b473-d39c5d64cf56" :answer/id #uuid "5fb66c80-1747-5cd6-b473-d39c5d64cf56" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9f7eb28b-e6d3-58a7-ad9a-3a6681ca1ee4" :question/id #uuid "9f7eb28b-e6d3-58a7-ad9a-3a6681ca1ee4" :question/math-academy-id "q-134125" :question/problem "![](images/4c/4c03191b325ee430f02216f35998db128ea99c6096222d489fa817f680e98d10.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows the relationship between the time some college students spend studying at night for their final exams and the number of hours they sleep per night. Which of the following is the best approximation of the correlation coefficient?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the linear correlation coefficient $\\rho $ satisfies the inequality\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\rho \\le 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\rho =1$ indicates a perfect positive correlation, and $\\rho =-1$ indicates a perfect negative correlation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our scatter plot.\n\n![](images/74/74aa691e42eea766595eede7d42d59e164509d6dbf3c8a609dd42e1196d11512.png)\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The points in the scatter plot seem to follow a straight line with a *negative* slope. So, the correlation coefficient must be *negative*. Thus, we disregard the following values:\n\n$$\n\\rho ≈0.8,\\,\\rho ≈0.4,\\,\\rho ≈0\n$$\n\n- The points are scattered quite far from the corresponding trend line. So, the correlation coefficient must be closer to $0.$ Thus, we disregard the following value:\n\n$$\n\\rho ≈-0.9\n$$\n\nTherefore, the best approximation of the correlation coefficient must be $\\rho ≈-0.3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f26454ee-b84b-57c9-bd6b-8ffaabec57a1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f26454ee-b84b-57c9-bd6b-8ffaabec57a1" :answer-field/id #uuid "f26454ee-b84b-57c9-bd6b-8ffaabec57a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fa53955d-d092-59ca-bedb-4ee2c6e6f178" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fa53955d-d092-59ca-bedb-4ee2c6e6f178" "answer-4dde781b-7d3c-5e90-88e4-b6e543af6cd0" "answer-f4ea6508-51c1-5ee1-974c-71ab52286e70" "answer-18de5bcf-ee53-583d-9955-d6382d059b1e" "answer-52f0bd21-8e5a-5e90-94c5-b78bb36bcb81"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa53955d-d092-59ca-bedb-4ee2c6e6f178" :answer/id #uuid "fa53955d-d092-59ca-bedb-4ee2c6e6f178" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\rho ≈-0.3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4dde781b-7d3c-5e90-88e4-b6e543af6cd0" :answer/id #uuid "4dde781b-7d3c-5e90-88e4-b6e543af6cd0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\rho ≈-0.9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f4ea6508-51c1-5ee1-974c-71ab52286e70" :answer/id #uuid "f4ea6508-51c1-5ee1-974c-71ab52286e70" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\rho ≈0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18de5bcf-ee53-583d-9955-d6382d059b1e" :answer/id #uuid "18de5bcf-ee53-583d-9955-d6382d059b1e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-52f0bd21-8e5a-5e90-94c5-b78bb36bcb81" :answer/id #uuid "52f0bd21-8e5a-5e90-94c5-b78bb36bcb81" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e6c59cf1-e1f3-5c09-adfe-bd7d9e53be52" :question/id #uuid "e6c59cf1-e1f3-5c09-adfe-bd7d9e53be52" :question/math-academy-id "q-134120" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9dd43d8833be95bd8b13a98b65dddd1272501599b107053296302cb7fae7e58b.png)\n\nWhich of the following is the best approximation of the correlation coefficient for the data in the scatter plot above?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the linear correlation coefficient $\\rho $ satisfies the inequality\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\rho \\le 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\rho =1$ indicates a perfect positive correlation, and $\\rho =-1$ indicates a perfect negative correlation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our scatter plot.\n\n![](images/ae/aecc569c03ed874748993b77ed09a8a318dae23bcaea09334d98af9da36d8a87.png)\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The points in the scatter plot seem to follow a straight line with a *positive* slope. So, the correlation coefficient must be *positive*. Thus, we disregard the following values:\n\n$$\n\\rho ≈-0.75,\\,\\rho ≈-0.3,\\,\\rho ≈0\n$$\n\n- The points lie pretty close to the corresponding trend line. So, the correlation coefficient must be closer to $1.$ Thus, we disregard the following value:\n\n$$\n\\rho ≈0.25\n$$\n\nTherefore, the best approximation of the correlation coefficient must be $\\rho ≈0.85.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-724bde99-b3bb-58df-81bd-552e8017bb6e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-724bde99-b3bb-58df-81bd-552e8017bb6e" :answer-field/id #uuid "724bde99-b3bb-58df-81bd-552e8017bb6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e71cb635-681e-51e0-8351-d1cc6f433ab1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-edd640cf-5862-5d11-9954-da14ddb5d70f" "answer-ffb7ea0c-c555-50e6-b6f4-54644915ff89" "answer-2042dc18-49bf-58fb-8