g) we obtain  ![](images/4f/4ffc15045d598404d6a76b86de375abc74e21c66821149760272d73eca6b3190.svg) and ![](images/2a/2a50ea8c8351cbd27e82b2c20da9a482d093b20e039a650930857b36700968b1.svg)\n\nAlso, notice that ![](images/8b/8b994afa2154f42fc39a361c03eb163e6ae83e588ebbe949028a0bc75e10b16d.svg) at both intersection points.\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of the point ![](images/75/7533ad047439b7d799dd7afee5d6a7bc9f4404fbadb8c3f27ef6d3952119ff6b.svg) are ![](images/ee/eece868ab8c5a66a3f640d9f98b6ce38ed8659c0eef8158a55c5ce4edc1a5157.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105763 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a5a55304-1327-53a7-ab60-6725a01ed1fd" "question-eaf14619-5650-5c49-bb13-301ef3e6d48c" "question-0d83a7e0-a78a-57e3-b959-267d62524fd8" "question-ed4d6b98-693b-555c-b503-e7cd998ff8be" "question-c975a336-1c2a-5428-a315-180711c9bbb4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105615 :question/problem "The circles $r=4\\cos \\theta$ and $r=4\\sqrt{3}\\sin  \\theta$ intersect at points $O$ and $A,$ as shown in the diagram. What are the polar coordinates of the point $A?$\n\n![](images/27/2766090535f1931a0cb15a248eb62c9b1dc5b5f9bc15b6dff5d544876527d77c.png)" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we notice from the graph that $(r,\\theta)=\\left(0, 0\\right)$ is an obvious first point of intersection. \n\nTo find the second point of intersection, we set the equations of the curves equal to each other and solve for $\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{4}{4\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the solution $\\theta =\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ in the $1$st quadrant.\n\nNow, we use the equation $r=4\\cos \\theta$ to find the $r$-coordinate of the point $A\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(r,\\theta)=\\left(2\\sqrt{3}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105762 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a30f6995-a7bb-57e9-9de6-99d42c4d49a3" "question-92072883-4631-52cf-ad3b-532b194d125c" "question-cdda3bdc-973b-5424-bda2-1798eb90cfd3" "question-2d565dfb-3434-5111-8bf3-8db207613208" "question-b30c011d-e3fe-5a6f-9c15-6828aaa923bb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105614 :question/problem "Find the polar coordinates of the intersection points of the polar curves  $r =3+3\\cos\\theta$ and $r = 4,$ shown below. Round your answers to $1$ decimal place where appropriate.\n\n![](images/cb/cbdc7d93d48f21d3d4ae67a5cb7692801d240e896263a137557e67f8a5bfbcfc.png)" :question/worked-solution "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=3+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solving for $\\theta.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t3+3\\cos \\theta & = 4\\[5pt]\n \t \t3\\cos \\theta & = 1   \\[3pt]\n \t\t\\cos \\theta & = \\dfrac 1 3.\n\t}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[0^\\circ,360^\\circ),$ we obtain $\\theta_1 \\approx 70.5^{\\circ}$ and $\\theta_2 \\approx 289.5,$ rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\nNotice that we must have \n$r=4$\n at any of the points of intersection.\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n(r,\\theta)=\\left(4 , 70.5 \\, ^\\circ \\right)\\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad (r,\\theta)=\\left(4 , 289.5 \\, ^\\circ\\right).\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186106318 :question/problem "![](images/2e/2ee08e17ee0f474a675e83275c290a29a0f2814e5277694a5629282cfd861d56.png)\n\nFind the polar coordinates for the intersection points of the circles $r=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $r=\\sqrt{3},$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solve for $\\theta .$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[0,2\\pi ),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ .\n\nAlso, notice that $r=\\sqrt{3}$ at both intersection points.\n\nTherefore, polar coordinates of the points of intersection are \n\n$\\,$ $(r,\\theta )=(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{4\\pi }{3})\\,$ and $\\,(r,\\theta )=(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186104056 :answer-field/correct "answer-8433a122-1303-5528-b61c-12e5dea05139" :answer-field/choices ["answer-16f8d075-a521-5559-bed6-a47dccce84fa" "answer-0d56e221-abf1-5229-9caf-3e3b47a4855e" "answer-8433a122-1303-5528-b61c-12e5dea05139" "answer-c9223afd-b370-594f-bf98-95ec84abd306" "answer-65f2c7e5-1aee-5f70-94f1-b52ebb032fd7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16f8d075-a521-5559-bed6-a47dccce84fa" :answer/id #uuid "16f8d075-a521-5559-bed6-a47dccce84fa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(2,\\frac{6\\pi }{5})$ and $(2,\\frac{9\\pi }{5})$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d56e221-abf1-5229-9caf-3e3b47a4855e" :answer/id #uuid "0d56e221-abf1-5229-9caf-3e3b47a4855e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(2,\\frac{7\\pi }{6})$ and $(2,\\frac{11\\pi }{6})$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8433a122-1303-5528-b61c-12e5dea05139" :answer/id #uuid "8433a122-1303-5528-b61c-12e5dea05139" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{4\\pi }{3})$ and $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3})$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c9223afd-b370-594f-bf98-95ec84abd306" :answer/id #uuid "c9223afd-b370-594f-bf98-95ec84abd306" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{4})$ and $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{7\\pi }{4})$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-65f2c7e5-1aee-5f70-94f1-b52ebb032fd7" :answer/id #uuid "65f2c7e5-1aee-5f70-94f1-b52ebb032fd7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{7\\pi }{6})$ and $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{11\\pi }{6})$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-47f83011-f0ac-5fe7-b577-1abb06b9a992" :question/id #uuid "47f83011-f0ac-5fe7-b577-1abb06b9a992" :question/math-academy-id "q-121755" :question/problem "![](images/f5/f556adb5f1af77ed89db28638a19051b6ebb8ff056eabe60a8a6357984011a12.png)\n\nThe circles $r=1$ and $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ intersect at points $A$ and $B,$ as shown above. What are the polar coordinates of the point $B?$" :question/worked-solution "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solving for $\\theta .$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[{0}^{∘},\\text{ }\\,{360}^{∘}),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}={60}^{∘}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}={300}^{∘}.$  \n\nAlso, notice that $r=1$ at both intersection points.\n\nTherefore, polar coordinates of the point $B$ are \n\n$\\,$ $(r,\\theta )=(1,{300}^{∘}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-dedbb997-f316-5f26-8b09-344ac2060a51"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-dedbb997-f316-5f26-8b09-344ac2060a51" :answer-field/id #uuid "dedbb997-f316-5f26-8b09-344ac2060a51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-bd1993ad-2046-5bbf-b084-3d5a7f7e6622" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bd1993ad-2046-5bbf-b084-3d5a7f7e6622" "answer-3fd77f70-e1a1-50cc-801e-9246f14fe6dc" "answer-bc3b5e0b-fabc-5514-a006-5d26116b1cea" "answer-de96cb1d-479d-57f8-8221-6b3c0e495910" "answer-45d2dac0-5bd8-5fa3-81ab-7b8d2b12b311"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bd1993ad-2046-5bbf-b084-3d5a7f7e6622" :answer/id #uuid "bd1993ad-2046-5bbf-b084-3d5a7f7e6622" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,{300}^{∘})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3fd77f70-e1a1-50cc-801e-9246f14fe6dc" :answer/id #uuid "3fd77f70-e1a1-50cc-801e-9246f14fe6dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,{330}^{∘})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bc3b5e0b-fabc-5514-a006-5d26116b1cea" :answer/id #uuid "bc3b5e0b-fabc-5514-a006-5d26116b1cea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,{300}^{∘})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-de96cb1d-479d-57f8-8221-6b3c0e495910" :answer/id #uuid "de96cb1d-479d-57f8-8221-6b3c0e495910" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,{315}^{∘})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45d2dac0-5bd8-5fa3-81ab-7b8d2b12b311" :answer/id #uuid "45d2dac0-5bd8-5fa3-81ab-7b8d2b12b311" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},{330}^{∘})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2156ef1c-7aad-5ba1-895f-5a7013935e5e" :question/id #uuid "2156ef1c-7aad-5ba1-895f-5a7013935e5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-325647" :question/problem "![](images/82/823ff7d93c17556b8587da62430c3168bbd6d49da43e88450f0804d83598f23f.png)\n\nThe circles $r=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $r=1$ intersect for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ at points $A$ and $B,$ as shown above. What are the polar coordinates $({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})$ and $({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})$ of the points $A$ and $B,$ respectively? Express the polar angles ${\\theta }_{A}$ and ${\\theta }_{B}$ in radians.\n\n$({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})=(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$),\\,$\n\n$({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})=(${{field-3}}$,$\n{{field-4}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solve for $\\theta .$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[0,2\\pi ),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$\n\nAlso, notice that $r=1$ at both intersection points.\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates $({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})$ and $({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})$ of the points $A$ and $B,$ respectively, are\n\n$$\n({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})=(1,\\frac{2\\pi }{3}),\\,({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})=(1,\\frac{4\\pi }{3}).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2e3de602-9b02-5e62-a90c-937f84dbf69b" "answer-field-7c973c0b-c17f-575d-ba6c-439b72f6ffd9" "answer-field-1438285a-c5cd-5a31-b715-b35536c03825" "answer-field-603497ab-4fdc-5c48-88e2-3ed5a2fed9cd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2e3de602-9b02-5e62-a90c-937f84dbf69b" :answer-field/id #uuid "2e3de602-9b02-5e62-a90c-937f84dbf69b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f94ae38d-ef49-51f3-be98-ef3bdb8f6786" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f94ae38d-ef49-51f3-be98-ef3bdb8f6786"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f94ae38d-ef49-51f3