id "q-120748" :question/problem "![](images/f7/f78441133b6c3ac196e9e9e8dcfbbbdef875468e54ee34a3509b26d7ed86578e.png)\n\nThe circles $r=2$ and $r=2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ intersect at points $A$ and $B,$ as shown above. What are the polar coordinates of the point $B?$" :question/worked-solution "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solving for $\\theta .$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[{0}^{∘},\\text{ }\\,{360}^{∘}),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}={45}^{∘}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}={135}^{∘}.$ \n\nAlso, notice that $r=2$ at both intersection points.\n\nTherefore, polar coordinates of the point $B$ are \n\n$\\,$ $(r,\\theta )=(2,{135}^{∘}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5e5baca4-797a-5167-9991-36c46ba93ec2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5e5baca4-797a-5167-9991-36c46ba93ec2" :answer-field/id #uuid "5e5baca4-797a-5167-9991-36c46ba93ec2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8e8446d6-878c-53e5-8dc5-9f23ed0b752e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-70730940-311e-5ac8-82e7-b732c8d7b04b" "answer-8e8446d6-878c-53e5-8dc5-9f23ed0b752e" "answer-25fa1f4b-97ef-5790-af1e-168d7931db47" "answer-f603c36e-3fc5-5979-b39d-11d91b66908a" "answer-6b601d25-f8de-5b76-b357-7333dea0a6be"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-70730940-311e-5ac8-82e7-b732c8d7b04b" :answer/id #uuid "70730940-311e-5ac8-82e7-b732c8d7b04b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,{120}^{∘})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8e8446d6-878c-53e5-8dc5-9f23ed0b752e" :answer/id #uuid "8e8446d6-878c-53e5-8dc5-9f23ed0b752e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,{135}^{∘})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-25fa1f4b-97ef-5790-af1e-168d7931db47" :answer/id #uuid "25fa1f4b-97ef-5790-af1e-168d7931db47" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,{30}^{∘})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f603c36e-3fc5-5979-b39d-11d91b66908a" :answer/id #uuid "f603c36e-3fc5-5979-b39d-11d91b66908a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},{135}^{∘})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6b601d25-f8de-5b76-b357-7333dea0a6be" :answer/id #uuid "6b601d25-f8de-5b76-b357-7333dea0a6be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},{330}^{∘})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-072c9b75-cb6d-571a-a32a-79cae44e115d" :question/id #uuid "072c9b75-cb6d-571a-a32a-79cae44e115d" :question/math-academy-id "q-323087" :question/problem "![](images/ec/ec1381a50c493fce0f5017e706251c68c1a8a20ba5b68ef268d5905aa20772e6.png)\n\nThe circles $r=4\\sqrt{2}$ and $r=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ intersect at points $A$ and $B,$ as shown above. What are the polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ of the point $A?$ Express the polar angle $\\theta \\in [{0}^{∘},\\text{ }\\,{360}^{∘})$ in degrees.\n\n$(r,\\theta )=(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘})$" :question/worked-solution "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solving for $\\theta .$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =4\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[{0}^{∘},\\text{ }\\,{360}^{∘}),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}={45}^{∘}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}={135}^{∘}.$ \n\nAlso, notice that $r=4\\sqrt{2}$ at both intersection points.\n\nTherefore, polar coordinates of the point $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ $(r,\\theta )=(4\\sqrt{2},{135}^{∘}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-44ef42ba-8904-590e-855b-cefdf44b2505" "answer-field-ece49f7d-e08d-5601-b02d-77ccc0ccc997"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-44ef42ba-8904-590e-855b-cefdf44b2505" :answer-field/id #uuid "44ef42ba-8904-590e-855b-cefdf44b2505" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-c6d2cea6-2dfb-5470-bdb9-b125b8517442" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c6d2cea6-2dfb-5470-bdb9-b125b8517442"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c6d2cea6-2dfb-5470-bdb9-b125b8517442" :answer/id #uuid "c6d2cea6-2dfb-5470-bdb9-b125b8517442" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ece49f7d-e08d-5601-b02d-77ccc0ccc997" :answer-field/id #uuid "ece49f7d-e08d-5601-b02d-77ccc0ccc997" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ac5c1f87-7097-5e55-8673-290f95420717" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ac5c1f87-7097-5e55-8673-290f95420717"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ac5c1f87-7097-5e55-8673-290f95420717" :answer/id #uuid "ac5c1f87-7097-5e55-8673-290f95420717" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "135" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a5a55304-1327-53a7-ab60-6725a01ed1fd" :question/id #uuid "a5a55304-1327-53a7-ab60-6725a01ed1fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-121800" :question/problem "![](images/9e/9e7a57e93473508aa49868e75769d905f52be9b3b6c74bfd9328b5e9bfe240c7.png)\n\nThe circles $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $r=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ intersect at points $O$ and $A,$ as shown above. What are the polar coordinates of the point $A?$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we notice from the graph that the origin $O$ is an obvious first point of intersection.\n\nTo find the second point of intersection, we set the equations of the curves equal to each other and solve for $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =-\\frac{2}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the solution $\\theta ={315}^{∘}$ in the $4$th quadrant.\n\nNow, we use the equation $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ to find the $r$-coordinate of the point $A:$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ $(r,\\theta )=(\\sqrt{2},{315}^{∘}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1b8e7f42-8da1-5cc1-8274-3f4351f120b2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1b8e7f42-8da1-5cc1-8274-3f4351f120b2" :answer-field/id #uuid "1b8e7f42-8da1-5cc1-8274-3f4351f120b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-287221ce-e666-58c8-b087-6ba9c8f66683" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e4068d4b-2d54-5c8e-b63e-f5f8c260fc9c" "answer-83ca30d5-bddc-58c6-8bb1-02f885a76084" "answer-50395f2a-ef0b-5443-adfd-eb8d58b5b772" "answer-287221ce-e666-58c8-b087-6ba9c8f66683" "answer-2d98655d-e11c-5e52-8f86-862670c63ae8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4068d4b-2d54-5c8e-b63e-f5f8c260fc9c" :answer/id #uuid "e4068d4b-2d54-5c8e-b63e-f5f8c260fc9c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,{315}^{∘})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83ca30d5-bddc-58c6-8bb1-02f885a76084" :answer/id #uuid "83ca30d5-bddc-58c6-8bb1-02f885a76084" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,{330}^{∘})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-50395f2a-ef0b-5443-adfd-eb8d58b5b772" :answer/id #uuid "50395f2a-ef0b-5443-adfd-eb8d58b5b772" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},{300}^{∘})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-287221ce-e666-58c8-b087-6ba9c8f66683" :answer/id #uuid "287221ce-e666-58c8-b087-6ba9c8f66683" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},{315}^{∘})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d98655d-e11c-5e52-8f86-862670c63ae8" :answer/id #uuid "2d98655d-e11c-5e52-8f86-862670c63ae8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},{330}^{∘})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-eaf14619-5650-5c49-bb13-301ef3e6d48c" :question/id #uuid "eaf14619-5650-5c49-bb13-301ef3e6d48c" :question/math-academy-id "q-324164" :question/problem "![](images/51/515266ff58d998d660634860a07f26e65e0a2791a19a87e1a0f0cd8b8839c9b0.png)\n\nThe circles $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $r=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ intersect at points $O$ and $A,$ as shown above. What are the polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ of the point $A?$ Express the polar angle $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ in radians.\n\n$(r,\\theta )=(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we notice from the graph that the origin $O$ is an obvious first point of intersection. \n\nTo find the second point of intersection, we set the equations of the curves equal to each other and solve for $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the solution $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ in the $1$st quadrant.\n\nNow, we use the equation $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ to find the $r$-coordinate of the point $A:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, polar coordinates of the point $A$ are \n\n$$\n(r,\\theta )=(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fa646c97-4258-5a7b-a5e5-2be260e50b9a" "answer-field-ed3bb5b2-9573-5648-9ac1-e2c7b129d109"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fa646c97-4258-5a7b-a5e5-2be260e50b9a" :answer-field/id #uuid "fa646c97-4258-5a7b-a5e5-2be260e50b9a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-dfbe5359-0657-5fe4-a2f2-94b550f4445f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dfbe5359-0657-5fe4-a2f2-94b550f4445f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dfbe5359-0657-5fe4-a2f2-94b550f4445f" :answer/id #uuid "dfbe5359-0657-5fe4-a2f2-94b550f4445f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ed3bb5b2-9573-5648-9ac1-e2c7b129d109" :answer-field/id #uuid "ed3bb5b2-9573-5648-9ac1-e2c7b129d109" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-8b77ba13-51d9-5349-b156-c9977d72aec8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8b77ba13-51d9-5349-b156-c9977d72aec8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b77ba13-51d9-5349-b156-c9977d72aec8" :answer/id #uuid "8b77ba13-51d9-5349-b156-c9977d72aec8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0d83a7e0-a78a-57e3-b959-267d62524fd8" :question/id #uuid "0d83a7e0-a78a-57e3-b959-267d62524fd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-324152" :question/problem "![](images/59/59ad56e7eae8e8598ebd511a7b499f35cd6a178405f8198831b23e8d5d47a513.png)\n\nThe circles $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $  intersect at points $O$ and $A,$ as shown above. What are the polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ of the point $A?$ Express the polar angle $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ in radians.\n\n$(r,\\theta )=(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we notice from the graph that the origin $O$ is an obvious first point of intersection. \n\nTo find the second point of intersection, we set the equations of the curves equal to each other and solve for $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the solution $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ in the $1$st quadrant.\n\nNow, we use the equation $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ to find the $r$-coordinate of the point $A:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, polar coordinates of the point $A$ are \n\n \n\n$$\n(r,\\theta )=(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4cafba33-0210-5ea7-a371-41e466aabf80" "answer-field-c14e8247-0744-5637-b0b4-5ca12a64d825"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4cafba33-0210-5ea7-a371-41e466aabf80" :answer-field/id #uuid "4cafba33-0210-5ea7-a371-41e466aabf80" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-bddb17aa-bb31-5167-aec7-5f150d00bbdc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bddb17aa-bb31-5167-aec7-5f150d00bbdc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bddb17aa-bb31-5167-aec7-5f150d00bbdc" :answer/id #uuid "bddb17aa-bb31-5167-aec7-5f150d00bbdc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c14e8247-0744-5637-b0b4-5ca12a64d825" :answer-field/id #uuid "c14e8247-0744-5637-b0b4-5ca12a64d825" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-951aa75b-480f-5a6a-85e6-78d9055600b7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-951aa75b-480f-5a6a-85e6-78d9055600b7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-951aa75b-480f-5a6a-85e6-78d9055600b7" :answer/id #uuid "951aa75b-480f-5a6a-85e6-78d9055600b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ed4d6b98-693b-555c-b503-e7cd998ff8be" :question/id #uuid "ed4d6b98-693b-555c-b503-e7cd998ff8be" :question/math-academy-id "q-41666" :question/problem "![](images/3c/3cd5ba688d516f05dbb9c8aa43626ec3194c384034ab612084da5dcc67a14ce6.png)\n\nThe circles $r=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $r=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ intersect at points $O$ and $A,$ as shown above. What are the polar coordinates of the point $A?$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we notice from the graph that the origin $O$ is an obvious first point of intersection.\n\nTo find the second point of intersection, we set the equations of the curves equal to each other and solve for $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the solution $\\theta ={60}^{∘}$ in the $1$st quadrant.\n\nNow, we use the equation $r=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ to find the $r$-coordinate of the point $A:$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡({60}^{∘}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ $(r,\\theta )=(\\frac{3}{2},{60}^{∘}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9cb4aa30-610b-59c9-972c-ebd2569116be"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9cb4aa30-610b-59c9-972c-ebd2569116be" :answer-field/id #uuid "9cb4aa30-610b-59c9-972c-ebd2569116be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-bb7f3cbf-3937-5e35-953e-49d18785c0fa" :answer-field/choices ["answer-08ad9ece-6e98-5b75-949c-dce0b3e878c0" "answer-bb7f3cbf-3937-5e35-953e-49d18785c0fa" "answer-114f1305-c460-5d93-8f4a-d76eb9b5f8d8" "answer-421cfb40-fd75-524d-8050-661957d0bddf" "answer-789e6d88-34e4-5b15-b0c8-2d19410bc047"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-08ad9ece-6e98-5b75-949c-dce0b3e878c0" :answer/id #uuid "08ad9ece-6e98-5b75-949c-dce0b3e878c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},{55}^{∘})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bb7f3cbf-3937-5e35-953e-49d18785c0fa" :answer/id #uuid "bb7f3cbf-3937-5e35-953e-49d18785c0fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},{60}^{∘})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-114f1305-c460-5d93-8f4a-d76eb9b5f8d8" :answer/id #uuid "114f1305-c460-5d93-8f4a-d76eb9b5f8d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},{70}^{∘})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-421cfb40-fd75-524d-8050-661957d0bddf" :answer/id #uuid "421cfb40-fd75-524d-8050-661957d0bddf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{4}{3},{60}^{∘})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-789e6d88-34e4-5b15-b0c8-2d19410bc047" :answer/id #uuid "789e6d88-34e4-5b15-b0c8-2d19410bc047" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{3},{60}^{∘})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c975a336-1c2a-5428-a315-180711c9bbb4" :question/id #uuid "c975a336-1c2a-5428-a315-180711c9bbb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-324172" :question/problem "![](images/ce/ce263ef057316e67e65b3adf1a5d66797c6a97c9bdcd33cae866d9111e523e10.png)\n\nThe curves $r=\\sqrt{3}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $r=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ intersect for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ at points $A$ and $B,$ as shown above. What are the polar coordinates $({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})$ and $({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})$ of the points $A$ and $B,$ respectively? Express the polar angles ${\\theta }_{A}$ and ${\\theta }_{B}$ in radians.\n\n$({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})=($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$),\\,$\n\n$({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})=($\n{{field-3}}$,$\n{{field-4}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=\\sqrt{3}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solve for $\\theta .$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\sqrt{3}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[0,2\\pi ),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$\n\nNow, we use the equation $r=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ to find the $r$-coordinates of the intersection points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1} & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})=-4\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {r}_{2} & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})=-4\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates $({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})$ and $({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})$ of the points $A$ and $B,$ respectively, are\n\n$$\n({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})=(2\\sqrt{3},\\frac{4\\pi }{3}),\\,({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})=(2\\sqrt{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-90685216-2313-5ba6-972a-4dedde848c5e" "answer-field-3fd15637-13c3-5b02-a6e6-7167a6179c28" "answer-field-564db509-6137-5b97-b4e7-601a8c1e8728" "answer-field-fd614a72-9c5a-56ae-accf-fea2114cc61f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-90685216-2313-5ba6-972a-