ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f429073a-dfbf-5e35-999b-44ef38ac203b" :answer/id #uuid "f429073a-dfbf-5e35-999b-44ef38ac203b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a30f6995-a7bb-57e9-9de6-99d42c4d49a3" :question/id #uuid "a30f6995-a7bb-57e9-9de6-99d42c4d49a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-324194" :question/problem "The curves $r=2-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $r=-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ intersect at a single point in the third quadrant. Determine the polar coordinates of this point. Express the polar angle $\\theta $ in radians.\n\n$(r,\\theta )=(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=2-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solve for $\\theta .$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2 & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[0,2\\pi ),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the $\\theta $-coordinate of the point that we're interested in is $\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3},$ since it lies in the third quadrant.\n\nNow, we use the equation $r=-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ to find the $r$-coordinate of our point:\n\n$$\nr=-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})=\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of the desired intersection point are\n\n$\\,$ $(r,\\theta )=(\\frac{7}{2},\\text{ }\\frac{4\\pi }{3}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-417ba2b3-92db-5f71-9eb2-99d78f38ddf7" "answer-field-1789f9e8-fa16-5937-94c1-1a6d7f4887e0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-417ba2b3-92db-5f71-9eb2-99d78f38ddf7" :answer-field/id #uuid "417ba2b3-92db-5f71-9eb2-99d78f38ddf7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-54e10cfb-4665-5024-a9c4-1624bf7688ea" :answer-field/choices ["answer-54e10cfb-4665-5024-a9c4-1624bf7688ea"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-54e10cfb-4665-5024-a9c4-1624bf7688ea" :answer/id #uuid "54e10cfb-4665-5024-a9c4-1624bf7688ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1789f9e8-fa16-5937-94c1-1a6d7f4887e0" :answer-field/id #uuid "1789f9e8-fa16-5937-94c1-1a6d7f4887e0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-cd45d89c-18f2-5af8-af3a-72cc7f53e8f9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cd45d89c-18f2-5af8-af3a-72cc7f53e8f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd45d89c-18f2-5af8-af3a-72cc7f53e8f9" :answer/id #uuid "cd45d89c-18f2-5af8-af3a-72cc7f53e8f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-92072883-4631-52cf-ad3b-532b194d125c" :question/id #uuid "92072883-4631-52cf-ad3b-532b194d125c" :question/math-academy-id "q-121831" :question/problem "![](images/b8/b80f9b57f7ed0e79a9ebbe387d5cedc1a46e23da2d498599bc48ee52c76ff190.png)\n\nFind the polar coordinates of the intersection points of the polar curves  $r=2-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $r=1,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,r & =2-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\,r & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solve for $\\theta .$ This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘}),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}={30}^{∘}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}={150}^{∘}.$  \n\nNotice that we must have $r=1$ for any point that lies on both curves. \n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of the points of intersection are\n\n$\\,$ $(r,\\theta )=(1,{30}^{∘})\\,$ and $\\,(r,\\theta )=(1,{150}^{∘}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-aa11ec00-ff11-56ba-9564-ddc523852282"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-aa11ec00-ff11-56ba-9564-ddc523852282" :answer-field/id #uuid "aa11ec00-ff11-56ba-9564-ddc523852282" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-81d95ec4-26fc-557f-81bd-a82a364c0ae8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-81d95ec4-26fc-557f-81bd-a82a364c0ae8" "answer-991f7fc3-fdd7-51ab-9099-ff9096589f5b" "answer-a44f1b45-7e6e-5e5c-ba1f-31f87526a464" "answer-7b8a8462-9d74-596a-9456-548160d90875" "answer-7df40776-420d-5e36-a8f3-ab3b8b4797bc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-81d95ec4-26fc-557f-81bd-a82a364c0ae8" :answer/id #uuid "81d95ec4-26fc-557f-81bd-a82a364c0ae8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(1,{30}^{∘})$ and $(1,{150}^{∘})$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-991f7fc3-fdd7-51ab-9099-ff9096589f5b" :answer/id #uuid "991f7fc3-fdd7-51ab-9099-ff9096589f5b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(1,{45}^{∘})$ and $(1,{135}^{∘})$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a44f1b45-7e6e-5e5c-ba1f-31f87526a464" :answer/id #uuid "a44f1b45-7e6e-5e5c-ba1f-31f87526a464" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(1,{60}^{∘})$ and $(1,{120}^{∘})$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7b8a8462-9d74-596a-9456-548160d90875" :answer/id #uuid "7b8a8462-9d74-596a-9456-548160d90875" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(2,{30}^{∘})$ and $(2,{150}^{∘})$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7df40776-420d-5e36-a8f3-ab3b8b4797bc" :answer/id #uuid "7df40776-420d-5e36-a8f3-ab3b8b4797bc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(2,{60}^{∘})$ and $(2,{120}^{∘})$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-cdda3bdc-973b-5424-bda2-1798eb90cfd3" :question/id #uuid "cdda3bdc-973b-5424-bda2-1798eb90cfd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-121827" :question/problem "![](images/ca/caabcee019c7e97355b6a30f820872207e3f43245d97d3d09a44bf400628a975.png)\n\nFind the polar coordinates of the intersection points of the polar curves $r=3-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $r=4+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ shown above. Round your answers to $1$ decimal place where appropriate." :question/worked-solution "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=3-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=4+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solve for $\\theta .$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =4+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘}),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}≈{191.5}^{∘}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}≈348.5,$ rounded to $1$ decimal place. \n\nNow, we use the equation $r=4+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ to find the $r$-coordinates of the intersection points: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1} & =4+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{1}=4+3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{5})=\\frac{17}{5}=3.4 \\\\ {r}_{2} & =4+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{2}=4+3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{5})=\\frac{17}{5}=3.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, polar coordinates of the points of intersection are\n\n$\\,$ $(r,\\theta )≈(3.4,{191.5}^{∘})\\,$ and $\\,(r,\\theta )≈(3.4,{348.5}^{∘}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-883b05a3-cd95-5802-ba49-1d8e57c33615"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-883b05a3-cd95-5802-ba49-1d8e57c33615" :answer-field/id #uuid "883b05a3-cd95-5802-ba49-1d8e57c33615" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-20e83cd9-7cc6-5c6f-ac11-378a2d41900d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1489df57-7fc5-588f-96d3-c120cf10a7d1" "answer-570d7b76-2255-5c9a-bbf6-0130f9b43c35" "answer-6b6e0900-8fbd-5105-8ac6-daf150f9d1cd" "answer-20e83cd9-7cc6-5c6f-ac11-378a2d41900d" "answer-5dba6239-f541-5546-9927-92a968513a5b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1489df57-7fc5-588f-96d3-c120cf10a7d1" :answer/id #uuid "1489df57-7fc5-588f-96d3-c120cf10a7d1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(3.0,{197.5}^{∘})$ and $(3.0,{342.5}^{∘})$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-570d7b76-2255-5c9a-bbf6-0130f9b43c35" :answer/id #uuid "570d7b76-2255-5c9a-bbf6-0130f9b43c35" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(3.1,{189.6}^{∘})$ and $(3.1,{350.4}^{∘})$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6b6e0900-8fbd-5105-8ac6-daf150f9d1cd" :answer/id #uuid "6b6e0900-8fbd-5105-8ac6-daf150f9d1cd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(3.2,{194.3}^{∘})$ and $(3.2,{345.7}^{∘})$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-20e83cd9-7cc6-5c6f-ac11-378a2d41900d" :answer/id #uuid "20e83cd9-7cc6-5c6f-ac11-378a2d41900d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(3.4,{191.5}^{∘})$ and $(3.4,{348.5}^{∘})$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5dba6239-f541-5546-9927-92a968513a5b" :answer/id #uuid "5dba6239-f541-5546-9927-92a968513a5b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(3.5,{202.4}^{∘})$ and $(3.5,{337.6}^{∘})$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2d565dfb-3434-5111-8bf3-8db207613208" :question/id #uuid "2d565dfb-3434-5111-8bf3-8db207613208" :question/math-academy-id "q-324188" :question/problem "![](images/40/40db66aea0bf6de657ea7fa673ece434c01fa81ae0f7c3664cf9bf1cf8fa02e0.png)\n\nThe polar curves $r=5-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $r=1+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ intersect  for $\\theta \\in [{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘})$ at points $A$ and $B,$ as shown above. What are the polar coordinates $({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})$ and $({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})$ of the points $A$ and $B,$ respectively? Express the polar angles ${\\theta }_{A}$ and ${\\theta }_{B}$ in degrees.\n\n$({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})=(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘})$\n$\\,and\\,$ \n\n$({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})=(${{field-3}}$,$\n{{field-4}}${}^{∘})$" :question/worked-solution "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=5-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=1+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solve for $\\theta .$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =1+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =4 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘}),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}={30}^{∘}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}={150}^{∘}.$ \n\nNow, we use the equation $r=1+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ to find the $r$-coordinates of the intersection points: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1} & =1+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{1}=1+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=3 \\\\ {r}_{2} & =1+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{2}=1+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})=(3,{30}^{∘})\\,\\text{and }({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})=(3,{150}^{∘}).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ae6ca44a-c518-5e73-834b-2060d58c6c15" "answer-field-6aa915cc-8a9a-52e8-bb02-a4b5a41ab146" "answer-field-7b154a83-e57a-5f7e-a3fd-bfc3e77d8375" "answer-field-5133b675-4392-570e-9804-043317b0b23f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ae6ca44a-c518-5e73-834b-2060d58c6c15" :answer-field/id #uuid "ae6ca44a-c518-5e73-834b-2060d58c6c15" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-eccd2a69-62e6-5b27-b46d-fa183f6d99f8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-eccd2a69-62e6-5b27-b46d-fa183f6d99f8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eccd2a69-62e6-5b27-b46d-fa183f6d99f8" :answer/id #uuid "eccd2a69-62e6-5b27-b46d-fa183f6d99f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6aa915cc-8a9a-52e8-bb02-a4b5a41ab146" :answer-field/id #uuid "6aa915cc-8a9a-52e8-bb02-a4b5a41ab146" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-6860e7ff-a622-5842-b162-7d923e23d747" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6860e7ff-a622-5842-b162-7d923e23d747"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6860e7ff-a622-5842-b162-7d923e23d747" :answer/id #uuid "6860e7ff-a622-5842-b162-7d923e23d747" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "30" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7b154a83-e57a-5f7e-a3fd-bfc3e77d8375" :answer-field/id #uuid "7b154a83-e57a-5f7e-a3fd-bfc3e77d8375" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-97bff314-7fab-5f05-a6b8-ebcad67eb541" :answer-field/choices ["answer-97bff314-7fab-5f05-a6b8-ebcad67eb541"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-97bff314-7fab-5f05-a6b8-ebcad67eb541" :answer/id #uuid "97bff314-7fab-5f05-a6b8-ebcad67eb541" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5133b675-4392-570e-9804-043317b0b23f" :answer-field/id #uuid "5133b675-4392-570e-9804-043317b0b23f" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-5524124b-024f-5686-b0d5-bd2a7c84b8ec" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5524124b-024f-5686-b0d5-bd2a7c84b8ec"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5524124b-024f-5686-b0d5-bd2a7c84b8ec" :answer/id #uuid "5524124b-024f-5686-b0d5-bd2a7c84b8ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "150" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b30c011d-e3fe-5a6f-9c15-6828aaa923bb" :question/id #uuid "b30c011d-e3fe-5a6f-9c15-6828aaa923bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-121848" :question/problem "The curves $r=2-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ intersect at a single point in the second quadrant. Determine the polar coordinates of this point." :question/worked-solution "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=2-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solve for $\\theta .$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[0,2\\pi ),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\nTherefore, the $\\theta $-coordinate of the point that we're interested in is $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6},$ since it lies in the second quadrant.\n\nNow, we use the equation $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ to find the $r$-coordinate of our point:\n\n$$\nr=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of the desired intersection point are\n\n$\\,$ $(r,\\theta )=(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-00c32dba-43b3-51e4-90b4-f83a74a787dd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-00c32dba-43b3-51e4-90b4-f83a74a787dd" :answer-field/id #uuid "00c32dba-43b3-51e4-90b4-f83a74a787dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c4277d9a-1776-504e-a740-20236a450250" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c4277d9a-1776-504e-a740-20236a450250" "answer-5748d238-2d62-5c05-9b32-cc45a9cb23cb" "answer-9b661e8b-5a92-5a04-a768-7616735c352b" "answer-d5e6e99c-04e1-5531-ac7d-3bc194756e95" "answer-7a34109a-a5f7-5e0d-9dea-2408d0c9fe14"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c4277d9a-1776-504e-a740-20236a450250" :answer/id #uuid "c4277d9a-1776-504e-a740-20236a450250" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5748d238-2d62-5c05-9b32-cc45a9cb23cb" :answer/id #uuid "5748d238-2d62-5c05-9b32-cc45a9cb23cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{4\\pi }{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9b661e8b-5a92-5a04-a768-7616735c352b" :answer/id #uuid "9b661e8b-5a92-5a04-a768-7616735c352b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d5e6e99c-04e1-5531-ac7d-3bc194756e95" :answer/id #uuid "d5e6e99c-04e1-5531-ac7d-3bc194756e95" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{2\\pi }{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7a34109a-a5f7-5e0d-9dea-2408d0c9fe14" :answer/id #uuid "7a34109a-a5f7-5e0d-9dea-2408d0c9fe14" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186107116 :tutorial/content "It is often more convenient to use polar coordinates to determine the points of intersection of two curves. This is especially true if we are dealing with two circles. \n\nSuppose, for example, that we are given the circles ${\\color{}r=\\sqrt2\\,  {\\sin{\\theta}}}\\,$ and $\\, {\\color{}r=1},$ as shown in the figure below. How do we determine  the polar coordinates of the points of intersection of these two curves?  \n\n![](images/82/8225490259dfcf03d505b7f68a486a8246a46b29f6047f1c066d0e9436f8b55f.png)\n\n \n\nTo determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we must solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta\\mathbin{:}$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solving for $\\theta.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[0,2\\pi),$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}, \\qquad \\theta_2 = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}.\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $r=1$ at both intersection points. Therefore, the polar coordinates of the points of intersection are \n\n$$\n(r,\\theta)=\\bigg(1, \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\bigg)\\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad(r,\\theta)=\\bigg(1, \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}\\bigg).\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105953 :activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083228 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-fa22db6b-bdac-554c-ac94-acce50615822" "question-e2b9b16e-f138-5cec-b1bd-3819d0d7a9b5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083028 :question/problem "![](images/ac/ac3704f44a4719bf20040a66410c3ed4c9251a9a2c6775204d61c537ac9eaf83.png)\n\nA country keeps a record of its population growth. The scatter plot above shows the correspondence between the population (in millions) and the time (in years) since records began.\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate regression model for this data?\n\nI. Linear\n\nII. Quadratic\n\nIII. Exponential" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083229 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7decd470-9c6f-5eac-ae25-b28629130f6d" "question-be65dc10-b369-5d8a-bd2f-a7251513d699" "question-228f905a-60da-5b33-af12-e8ffff0a9be3" "question-a3b544e1-6c2a-5c64-b0a2-84d40a5c487b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083481 :question/problem "![](images/89/89aef4df3a3de7030d53a2fb2d6382087e9b744dbffa203fed521a230a00c6ba.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows a correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y.$ Which of the following is the most appropriate regression model for this data?" :question/worked-solution "It seems that the data points are scattered randomly. Therefore, there is no correlation." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186079781 :answer-field/correct 17592186079785 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-fa22db6b-bdac-554c-ac94-acce50615822" :question/id #uuid "fa22db6b-bdac-554c-ac94-acce50615822" :question/math-academy-id "q-10763" :question/problem "![](images/76/7687bdd3945e1ad655a2be00e9d661c0e3916c49424d908ae2a314885e4fcc76.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows a correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y.$ Which of the following is the most appropriate regression model for this data?" :question/worked-solution "As values of $x$ increase, the corresponding values of $y$ increase rapidly too. It seems like the points on the plot are scattered along an exponential curve, as shown below.\n\n![](images/4b/4bf51786207dbb75705d5e9406748e3a77c0cfe5a9e72144e6453bc7fb62bf3e.png)\n\nTherefore, among the given options, the most appropriate regression model is *exponential.*" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e5e29ad5-8429-5ccd-b63b-c47ea2c2bedf"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e5e29ad5-8429-5ccd-b63b-c47ea2c2bedf" :answer-field/id #uuid "e5e29ad5-8429-5ccd-b63b-c47ea2c2bedf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-235f3811-0302-5c45-8796-38d74e58c78e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-235f3811-0302-5c45-8796-38d74e58c78e" "answer-42ea96f4-da28-5b9b-a8a7-66e0814cfc8e" "answer-a287d588-d0f0-51b6-b637-65ac168828cb" "answer-d93e089f-288c-5722-b516-e89d3ab5e3ad" "answer-4e7e6ded-71bb-532d-98f5-96c39d62ea31"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-235f3811-0302-5c45-8796-38d74e58c78e" :answer/id #uuid "235f3811-0302-5c45-8796-38d74e58c78e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "exponential" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-42ea96f4-da28-5b9b-a8a7-66e0814cfc8e" :answer/id #uuid "42ea96f4-da28-5b9b-a8a7-66e0814cfc8e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "negative linear" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a287d588-d0f0-51b6-b637-65ac168828cb" :answer/id #uuid "a287d588-d0f0-51b6-b637-65ac168828cb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "no correlation" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d93e089f-288c-5722-b516-e89d3ab5e3ad" :answer/id #uuid "d93e089f-288c-5722-b516-e89d3ab5e3ad" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "positive linear" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4e7e6ded-71bb-532d-98f5-96c39d62ea31" :answer/id #uuid "4e7e6ded-71bb-532d-98f5-96c39d62ea31" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "quadratic" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083482 :question/worked-solution "As values of $x$ increase, the corresponding values of $y$ first increase and then decrease. It seems like the points on the plot are scattered along a parabola, as shown below.\n\n![](images/20/20acefe1f03a1ba3dd7154eb0ec9ab462009ed1153d7edb282807fac57130328.png)\n\nTherefore, among the given options, the most appropriate regression model is *quadratic.*" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186079787 :answer-field/correct 17592186079791 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-e2b9b16e-f138-5cec-b1bd-3819d0d7a9b5" :question/id #uuid "e2b9b16e-f138-5cec-b1bd-3819d0d7a9b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-17640" :question/problem "![](images/18/1862385f45f9082d3100246dbafd020c388c3fe4d83bc38e8c617916f79ab4a0.png)\n\nA drone is flying around some tourist buildings. The scatter plot above shows the correspondence between the drone's height, in meters, and the time, in minutes, since it started its journey. Which of the following is the most appropriate regression model for this data?" :question/worked-solution "As values of $x$ increase, the corresponding values of $y$ first increase and then decrease. It seems like the points on the plot are scattered along a parabola, as shown below.\n\n![](images/0a/0a00ddf1bea18a587a0a960a8965516a1cc3ff9ab9e13f25d6a23c59d51f44fc.png)\n\nTherefore, among the given options, the most appropriate regression model is *quadratic.*" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8a7f6bc7-349d-51b4-a153-e8e0a5b86236"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8a7f6bc7-349d-51b4-a153-e8e0a5b86236" :answer-field/id #uuid "8a7f6bc7-349d-51b4-a153-e8e0a5b86236" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b4228193-619c-5e23-af9b-e428892ac13b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-77cabf77-e6c0-5f6f-9a4c-bd41e979cb6f" "answer-6580bac7-1130-5351-893a-e4dcf60069b8" "answer-9f8618de-fdcf-5b2b-9940-636b7e597823" "answer-9a36e9ca-93f7-5a9a-a080-5f1b3ec3a340" "answer-b4228193-619c-5e23-af9b-e428892ac13b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-77cabf77-e6c0-5f6f-9a4c-bd41e979cb6f" :answer/id #uuid "77cabf77-e6c0-5f6f-9a4c-bd41e979cb6f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "exponential" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6580bac7-1130-5351-893a-e4dcf60069b8" :answer/id #uuid "6580bac7-1130-5351-893a-e4dcf60069b8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "negative linear" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f8618de-fdcf-5b2b-9940-636b7e597823" :answer/id #uuid "9f8618de-fdcf-5b2b-9940-636b7e597823" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "no correlation" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a36e9ca-93f7-5a9a-a080-5f1b3ec3a340" :answer/id #uuid "9a36e9ca-93f7-5a9a-a080-5f1b3ec3a340" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "positive linear" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b4228193-619c-5e23-af9b-e428892ac13b" :answer/id #uuid "b4228193-619c-5e23-af9b-e428892ac13b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "quadratic" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186233106 :question/worked-solution "As values of $x$ increase, the corresponding values of $y$ decrease rapidly, too. It seems like the points on the plot are scattered along an exponential curve, as shown below.\n\n![](images/3f/3f9d9f1f29a67bb46f2623786420efda71e4250589170727929077decb47d5c4.png)\n\nTherefore, among the given options, the most appropriate regression model is *exponential.*" :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-35ea3681-377a-5099-a577-b93581795e75"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-35ea3681-377a-5099-a577-b93581795e75" :answer-field/id #uuid "35ea3681-377a-5099-a577-b93581795e75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fe3b8d8c-73a6-5e7f-a586-582cda42d076" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fe3b8d8c-73a6-5e7f-a586-582cda42d076" "answer-035a38c1-817f-5159-b78e-5ab10d6d930f" "answer-169c14d0-40ec-5214-a466-aeed0a09193e" "answer-77ffc498-509a-5ba2-abb6-d360fe52df9f" "answer-029849fa-6481-5931-97f3-4a883e03f5da"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fe3b8d8c-73a6-5e7f-a586-582cda42d076" :answer/id #uuid "fe3b8d8c-73a6-5e7f-a586-582cda42d076" :answer/type :answer.type/text :a